// I0000+ — internal compiler errors (ICE). Unsuppressable (bypass lint
// overrides in diag_emit_range_notes). An ICE means a compiler invariant was
// violated — it should be unreachable from valid user input; if one fires, it
// is a compiler bug, never the user's fault.
// I0001: an unsolved metavariable / typevar reached a codegen chokepoint that
// must have a concrete type. The type-inference boundary check (E0301) is meant
// to catch every under-determined binding first, so this firing means a metavar
// escaped that net. Fails closed (never silently emits "Void").
pub const UNSOLVED_TYPEVAR_AT_CODEGEN = "I0001"
// I0002: a for-in target's runtime CT reached emit_for_in with no arm to handle it.
// The ForIn typecheck seam (NodeKind.ForIn, tc_check_node) rejects every non-iterable
// type before codegen runs, so this firing means the iterable IS one of the spec's
// built-in IntoIterator kinds (or the Channel receive extension) but codegen has not
// been armed for it yet — a missing-implementation bug, never the user's fault.
pub const UNHANDLED_ITERABLE_AT_CODEGEN = "I0002"
// I0003: an IR node of the wrong class reached the C printer (a statement kind where a
// value is required, or a value kind in statement position). The IR verifier rejects the
// shape before printing, so this firing means lowering handed the printer an unverified tree.
pub const MISPLACED_IR_KIND_AT_PRINT = "I0003"
// I0004: a code-generation stage that this build does not contain was reached. The
// tid-native emitters land stage by stage; until a stage lands, its entry point reports
// this. It reports and returns, like every diagnostic here, so the driver reads
// diag_ice_count before it uses the result -- otherwise an unbuilt stage looks like a
// program that legitimately emits nothing.
pub const CODEGEN_STAGE_NOT_BUILT = "I0004"
// The name recorded beside that code. Exported because the cg test harness pins a stage
// refusal by name, and a name written twice is a name a rename can leave behind: the tests
// would then count zero refusals and still compile.
pub const CODEGEN_STAGE_NOT_BUILT_NAME = "CodegenStageNotBuilt"
// I0005: a negative IR node id was handed to a producer that must embed it. The upstream
// stop was already reported, so this is not that failure a second time: it catches the
// CALLER that read the stop and carried on instead of propagating it. Embedding the id
// would put a node the pool does not hold inside a verified tree, so the seam refuses and
// blames the caller rather than the stage that stopped first.
pub const POISONED_IR_AT_SEAM = "I0005"
// I0006: an expression reached the source-text rebuilder with no rendering for its kind.
// The assert family reports the condition as the programmer wrote it, and nothing after
// the parser keeps that text, so it is rebuilt from the node. A kind with no rendering
// fails closed: inventing text would put words in a failure message that the program
// never contained, and the reader would trust them.
pub const NO_SOURCE_TEXT_FOR_EXPR = "I0006"
// I0007: the C printer was about to read a lowered tree the IR verifier refuses. The
// printer reads a tree by position and prints what it finds, so a defect lowering left
// behind becomes C that nothing later can tell is wrong. The driver checks every unit
// after lowering it and reports the first defect here instead of printing the unit. It
// reports what a stage that already stopped left behind only when no stage stopped: a
// second report over the wreckage of the first would bury the failure that caused it.
pub const UNVERIFIED_IR_AT_PRINT = "I0007"
// I0009: the build's rewrite of sealed ops qualified calls met a state it must never see: a
// second run on one program, a call it already rewrote, or a call with a declaration. A
// second rewrite would take the next argument as the receiver, so each one stops here.
pub const SEALED_OPS_REWRITE_INVARIANT = "I0009"
// I0010: a declared call reached codegen with an argument that binds to no parameter, or
// to one another argument already fills. Typecheck binds every argument by position or
// label first, so this firing means that binding and the call codegen reads disagree.
pub const CALL_ARG_UNBOUND_AT_CODEGEN = "I0010"
// I0011: arena promotion met a type state the front end must have refused or layout must
// never produce: a recursive type the walker reached (E0701 refuses those), a field boxed
// because it embeds its owner, or a word-inline map key that holds arena memory. Copying
// on would loop forever or leave an arena pointer in a value that outlives the arena.
pub const ARENA_PROMOTION_INVARIANT = "I0011"
// I0012: the parser's node pool or sublist stack met a state it can not hold: a limit of its
// packed layout, or a call out of order or out of bounds. The parser can not go on past it,
// so this one stops the compiler (diag_ice_abort) instead of returning. The message names
// the limit.
pub const PARSER_INTERNAL_LIMIT = "I0012"
pub const PARSER_INTERNAL_LIMIT_NAME = "ParserInternalLimit"
// I0013: the shared types header of a per-module build ended with a typedef still queued.
// A queued typedef reaches the C only when its pass drains the queue, and every unit takes
// the types header as written, so a block left there is a type each unit names and none
// declares. The driver stops here instead of handing cc a header that leaves it out.
pub const TYPEDEF_QUEUED_NOT_WRITTEN = "I0013"
// I0014: two declarations reached code generation under one C symbol. Name resolution
// rejects two declarations of one name in one module namespace, so a pair here is a C name
// the mangler gave twice. The driver stops instead of handing cc a unit that defines one
// symbol twice, or one where a call reaches whichever of the two C kept.
pub const TWO_DECLARATIONS_ONE_C_SYMBOL = "I0014"

// ── I0001 mangler sentinels ──────────────────────────────
//
// A type-name speller (tc_tid_to_c_tag, type_name_from_ct, …) has no node to anchor a
// diagnostic to and no `! Diag.Report` in its signature, so it cannot raise I0001 itself.
// Instead it returns one of these sentinels, and the LOCATED I0001 is raised downstream, at
// the two chokepoints that do have a node —
// mono_args_has_void_seg (src/codegen_expr.bl) and resolve_mono_slot
// (src/codegen_stmt.bl) — both of which detect a sentinel via diag_is_ice_seg.
//
// DO NOT rely on these failing closed if they escape a chokepoint.
// BLINK_I0001_erased_slot is a perfectly legal C identifier. Loudness is POSITION-DEPENDENT:
// as a field TYPE it is a hard cc error ("unknown type name"), but inside a mangled SYMBOL
// name it would compile and link silently — the way a collapsed symbol like
// blink_GKV_Void_Void would. The chokepoints are the guarantee; the spelling is only a
// greppable marker. Whether a chokepoint-free path to a mangled name exists is not
// established; until it is, treat an escaped sentinel as potentially silent.
//
// WHY THESE EXIST AT ALL: without this split, all four conditions below, plus a GENUINE
// Void, would spell the single string "Void". The two chokepoints would then have to
// string-key on "Void", which DECISIONS.md:342 (6-0) forbids — the tripwire "must key on
// the *kind*, not the resulting \"Void\" tag, so a genuine GKV[Void,Void] is unaffected".
// Without the split a genuine Box[Void] would raise I0001 too. Splitting the sentinels off
// leaves "Void" meaning ONLY genuine Void (decisions/under-determined-types.md:38-42 —
// Void is "an ordinary INHABITED, encodable type").
//
// NOT YET armed: the LOWERCASE twin "void" carries the same
// triple meaning in tc_tid_tag_at's TAGPOS_TOP and TAGPOS_INNER arms (the merged home of
// the former tc_tid_scalar_tag / tc_tid_inner_tag). Result[Void, Str] must still spell
// Result_void_str, matched at src/codegen_types.bl — but that string comes from the
// TAGPOS_TOP arm's explicit TyKind.Void case, not its unhandled-kind fall-through, so the
// fall-through (and the out-of-range guard) CAN be armed to these same sentinels without
// touching Result_void_str. See the comment on tc_tid_tag_at's TyKind.Void arm for the trace,
// and the "Collapse, never interpolate" comment on its TyKind.Option arm for the
// collapse-not-interpolate rule these arms must also follow.

// An unsolved metavariable / typevar (TyKind.Unknown) reached a speller. Bytes unchanged from
// the literal this const replaced, so every emitted symbol is byte-identical.
pub const ICE_SEG_UNSOLVED_TYPEVAR = "BLINK_I0001_unsolved_typevar_at_codegen"
// A tid whose kind has no arm in the speller (TyKind.Fn, any future kind). Was
// indistinguishable from a genuine Void.
pub const ICE_SEG_UNHANDLED_KIND = "BLINK_I0001_unhandled_kind"
// A negative or out-of-pool tid reached a speller. Was indistinguishable from a genuine Void.
pub const ICE_SEG_TID_OUT_OF_RANGE = "BLINK_I0001_tid_out_of_range"
// ERASURE: a type-arg slot that no producer could name. The string producers' old "Void"
// fallback — the spelling that made blink_GKV_Void_Void a valid C identifier which compiled
// and linked while collapsing sibling instantiations onto one typedef.
pub const ICE_SEG_ERASED_SLOT = "BLINK_I0001_erased_slot"
// A struct/enum tid that no declaration node answers for. A name cannot be spelled for a
// type that was never declared, so the speller fails closed instead of guessing a stem.
pub const ICE_SEG_NO_DECL = "BLINK_I0001_no_decl_for_type"
// An instance tid names its base by BARE name only; when two modules declared that
// name the tid cannot say which one it instantiates.
pub const ICE_SEG_AMBIGUOUS_DECL = "BLINK_I0001_ambiguous_decl_for_type"
// An expression kind the source-text rebuilder cannot write. Named like the other
// sentinels so a refusal that escapes a caller is visible in the output rather than
// reading as text the programmer wrote.
pub const ICE_SEG_NO_SOURCE_TEXT = "BLINK_I0001_no_source_text_for_expr"

// Whether a spelled type-name segment is one of the I0001 sentinels above — i.e. a
// speller gave up. The two I0001 chokepoints use this instead of comparing against
// "Void".
//
// This is a STRING test inside a change that is otherwise about kind-keying, which looks
// like a contradiction. It is not: BLINK_I0001_ is a mangler-INTERNAL prefix that no producer
// writes for any other purpose, whereas "Void" is a real type's real name — that is the whole
// difference. Do not read this as a precedent for string-keying a diagnostic on a type NAME;
// this predicate deliberately does not do that.
//
// NOT "unspoofable". `type BLINK_I0001_erased_slot {}` is legal Blink, and a user type by
// that name would make this predicate fire on a legitimate segment. Nobody writes that by
// accident, so it is not worth defending against — but do not build anything on top of a
// guarantee that is not there. To make it real the prefix would have to contain a
// character no Blink identifier can hold.
pub fn diag_is_ice_seg(seg: Str) -> Bool {
    seg.starts_with("BLINK_I0001_")
}

// Brackets after a value read as a type-argument list, so someone who meant indexing gets a
// type diagnostic. This help names the repair that exists for what they meant.
pub const ELEMENT_ACCESS_HELP = "Blink has no index operator; use `.get(...)` (§2.6)"

pub const VARIANT_FIELD_DEFAULT_HELP = "remove `=` and the value; a variant literal gives every field (§3.4)"

// ── Diagnostic struct ────────────────────────────────────────────────

pub type DiagEntry {
    severity: Str
    name: Str
    code: Str
    message: Str
    file: Str
    line: Int
    col: Int
    help: Str
    end_line: Int
    end_col: Int
    fix_action: Str
    fix_text: Str
    // Where the fix's edit goes. 0 means the diagnostic's own span: a fix may edit another
    // line, such as the `let` that a write on a later line needs to be `mut`.
    fix_line: Int
    fix_col: Int
    fix_end_line: Int
    fix_end_col: Int
    notes: List[Str]
    // A repair a person applies by hand, such as fields to fill in: it inserts text at one
    // point. The language server offers it under a kind that "fix all" never applies, and
    // `blink fix` ignores it, because the text it leaves is not yet a program.
    assist_action: Str = ""
    assist_text: Str = ""
    assist_line: Int = 0
    assist_col: Int = 0
    // Printed after the carets: what the marked text does, where the message says what is
    // wrong with it.
    label: Str = ""
}

type FileRange {
    start: Int
    end: Int
    path: Str
}

// Where a literal left open to the end of its file starts. Everything the lexer read after it
// was the literal's text, so what a later stage says about that text is a follow-on of the
// one lexer error.
type LexCutoff {
    file: Str
    line: Int
    col: Int
}

// What diag_record drops instead of recording, and what diag_flush does not print again.
// Reset per compile in diag_reset.
type DiagFilter {
    // Dedup byte-identical diagnostics. The two full passes over a fn body
    // (nr + tc) both call infer_type, which emits, so a single erroring node fires
    // twice. Keyed on the full tuple (severity|code|file|line|col|message) so
    // genuinely distinct diagnostics on the same node survive.
    seen: Map[Str, Int]
    lex_cutoffs: List[LexCutoff]
    // How many entries diag_flush has printed. A build flushes after its front end and again
    // after its back end, and each entry must print once.
    flushed: Int
}

pub let mut diag_entries: List[DiagEntry] = []
let mut file_ranges: List[FileRange] = []
let mut source_lines_cache: Map[Str, List[Str]] = Map()
let mut diag_filter: DiagFilter = DiagFilter { seen: Map(), lex_cutoffs: [], flushed: 0 }

// ── Configuration ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

pub let mut diag_format: Int = 0       // 0=human, 1=json
pub let mut diag_source_file: Str = ""
pub let mut diag_count: Int = 0
pub let mut diag_warn_count: Int = 0
pub let mut diag_ice_count: Int = 0
type DiagIceState {
    // How many internal errors were RENDERED, which is what the summary line reports. It
    // differs from diag_ice_count whenever one fault is reported more than once: the count a
    // caller reads to decide whether a result is usable must move on every report, and a
    // reader must still be shown the fault once.
    rendered: Int
    // What a command prints on stdout when diag_ice_abort stops the compiler, byte for byte,
    // or "".
    abort_stdout: Str
    // The document and the open request of the language server check in progress, or "" and
    // None. diag_ice_abort writes their frames from the fault itself.
    abort_lsp_uri: Str
    abort_lsp_request: Option[Int]
}
let mut diag_ice_state: DiagIceState = DiagIceState { rendered: 0, abort_stdout: "", abort_lsp_uri: "", abort_lsp_request: None }

// `blink check` sets its status line here, so a check --json that stops on an internal error
// still ends with one.
pub fn diag_set_abort_status_line(line: Str) {
    let text = if line == "" { "" } else { line.concat("\n") }
    diag_ice_state.abort_stdout = text
}

// The language server names the check it starts here, and clears it with "" and None when the
// check ends, so an editor learns of the internal error that ends the server.
pub fn diag_set_abort_lsp(uri: Str, request_id: Option[Int]) {
    diag_ice_state.abort_lsp_uri = uri
    diag_ice_state.abort_lsp_request = request_id
}
pub let mut diag_module_files: Map[Str, Str] = Map()

pub fn diag_register_file_range(start: Int, end: Int, path: Str) {
    file_ranges.push(FileRange { start: start, end: end, path: path })
}

// ── Lint severity overrides from [lints] in blink.toml ───────────────
let mut lint_overrides: Map[Str, Str] = Map()

// ── Per-function @allow suppression set ─────────────────────────────
let mut diag_allow_set: Map[Str, Int] = Map()

pub fn diag_push_allows(fn_node: Int) {
    let allow_ann = get_annotation(fn_node, "allow")
    if allow_ann == -1 {
        return
    }
    let args_sl = node_args(allow_ann)
    if args_sl == -1 {
        return
    }
    let mut i = 0
    while i < sublist_length(args_sl) {
        let arg_name = node_name(sublist_get(args_sl, i))
        diag_allow_set.insert(arg_name, 1)
        i = i + 1
    }
}

pub fn diag_clear_allows() {
    diag_allow_set.clear()
}

// The functions being walked right now, innermost last. An allow set belongs to the function
// it is written on, so a walk that reaches a nested function has to hand the enclosing one's
// set back on the way out. Clearing on the way out instead would leave the rest of the outer
// body unsuppressed, which is why the pairing below rebuilds rather than blanks.
let mut diag_allow_stack: List[Int] = []

pub fn diag_enter_allows(fn_node: Int) {
    diag_allow_stack.push(fn_node)
    diag_push_allows(fn_node)
}

pub fn diag_exit_allows() {
    if diag_allow_stack.len() == 0 { return }
    diag_allow_stack.pop()
    diag_allow_set = Map()
    for f in diag_allow_stack { diag_push_allows(f) }
}

// ── Lint override helpers ────────────────────────────────────────────

// The section 9.1 list. A diagnostic belongs on it only when silently suppressing it
// would destroy a record the language promises exists, which is why the two channels that
// record nothing do not reach it. Keyed by name and by code because [lints] accepts either.
pub fn diag_is_audit_gated(key: Str) -> Int {
    if key == "RawBypassesParam" { return 1 }
    if key == RAW_BYPASSES_PARAM { return 1 }
    // The old spelling. Until an unknown @allow or [lints] name is an error (E0842), dropping
    // this line would turn an @allow that copies it into a silent no-op.
    if key == "UnauditedFfi" { return 1 }
    if key == "UnauditedFFI" { return 1 }
    if key == UNAUDITED_FFI { return 1 }
    0
}

pub fn diag_audit_gated_help(key: Str) -> Str {
    "`{key}` records an audit, so it is suppressed only by `@trusted(audit: \"AUDIT-ID\")` on the enclosing function"
}

pub fn lint_set_override(name: Str, level: Str) ! Diag.Report {
    if diag_is_audit_gated(name) != 0 {
        diag_error_no_loc("AuditGatedSuppression", AUDIT_GATED_SUPPRESSION,
            "`{name}` cannot be set from [lints] in blink.toml",
            diag_audit_gated_help(name))
        return
    }
    lint_overrides.insert(name, level)
}

// The [lints] levels in force, one "name=level" line each. A level decides whether a
// diagnostic the emit reports is shown at all, so a record of what a build's emit read
// names them.
pub fn lint_override_lines() -> List[Str] {
    let mut out: List[Str] = []
    for k in lint_overrides.keys() {
        out.push("{k}={lint_overrides.get(k) ?? \"\"}")
    }
    out
}

// E1113-E1126. The spec lets no setting suppress or demote a lexer error (sections/02_syntax.md
// §2.4, "Recovery"): the token the lexer recovers with is not the text the author wrote, so a
// build that went on without the error would compile something else.
pub fn diag_is_lexer_code(code: Str) -> Bool {
    if code.byte_len() != 5 || !code.starts_with("E11") { return false }
    let tens = code.byte_at(3).to_int() - 48
    let ones = code.byte_at(4).to_int() - 48
    if tens < 0 || tens > 9 || ones < 0 || ones > 9 { return false }
    let n = tens * 10 + ones
    n >= 13 && n <= 26
}

fn lint_apply_override(severity: Str, name: Str, code: Str) -> Str {
    if diag_is_lexer_code(code) { return severity }
    // The literal has taken its type already, so a build that went on would run a value cut
    // to the type's width (sections/03_types.md §3.2 *Integer literal range*).
    if code == INT_LITERAL_OUT_OF_RANGE { return severity }
    if code == INVALID_LINT_LEVEL { return severity }
    // Neither channel records anything, so neither reaches an audit-gated diagnostic. Both
    // write sites refuse one outright; this is the second half of the same rule, so a name
    // that arrives here by some other route still fails to silence it.
    if diag_is_audit_gated(name) != 0 { return severity }
    if diag_allow_set.contains_key(name) != 0 {
        return "off"
    }
    // [lints] keys may be PascalCase names or W-codes (spec §4.16.8).
    let mut level = lint_overrides.get(name) ?? ""
    if level == "" {
        level = lint_overrides.get(code) ?? ""
    }
    if level == "" {
        return severity
    }
    if level == "off" {
        return "off"
    }
    if level == "error" && severity == "warning" {
        return "error"
    }
    if level == "warn" && severity == "error" {
        return "warning"
    }
    severity
}

pub fn embedded_inner_key(path: Str) -> Str {
    path.byte_slice(10, path.byte_len() - 1)
}

pub fn diag_normalize_path(path: Str) -> Str {
    match path.byte_index_of("lib/std/", 0) {
        Some(idx) => {
            if idx > 0 {
                return path.byte_slice(idx, path.byte_len())
            }
        }
        None => {}
    }
    match path.byte_index_of("lib/pkg/", 0) {
        Some(idx2) => {
            if idx2 > 0 {
                return path.byte_slice(idx2, path.byte_len())
            }
        }
        None => {}
    }
    if path.starts_with("<embedded:") && path.ends_with(">") {
        return "lib/{embedded_inner_key(path)}.bl"
    }
    path
}

// ── Emit helpers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn diag_emit(severity: Str, name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, line: Int, col: Int, help: Str) ! Diag.Report {
    diag_emit_range(severity, name, code, message, line, col, 0, 0, help)
}

pub fn diag_set_last_fix(action: Str, text: Str) {
    diag_set_last_fix_at(action, text, 0, 0, 0, 0)
}

// A fix whose edit replaces line:col..end_line:end_col of the diagnostic's file, not the
// diagnostic's own span.
pub fn diag_set_last_fix_at(action: Str, text: Str, line: Int, col: Int, end_line: Int, end_col: Int) {
    let idx = diag_entries.len() - 1
    if idx >= 0 {
        let d = diag_entries.get(idx).unwrap()
        diag_entries.set(idx, DiagEntry {
            fix_action: action, fix_text: text,
            fix_line: line, fix_col: col, fix_end_line: end_line, fix_end_col: end_col,
            ..d
        })
    }
}

pub fn diag_set_last_assist_at(action: Str, text: Str, line: Int, col: Int) {
    let idx = diag_entries.len() - 1
    if idx >= 0 {
        let d = diag_entries.get(idx).unwrap()
        diag_entries.set(idx, DiagEntry { assist_action: action, assist_text: text, assist_line: line, assist_col: col, ..d })
    }
}

pub fn diag_entry_count() -> Int {
    diag_entries.len()
}

// Whether a recorded fix with `action` in `file` starts at or after line `from_line`, before
// line:col, and has text that starts with `prefix`. `blink fix` applies every fix at once, so a
// fix that binds a new name must not take a name an earlier one binds.
pub fn diag_fix_before_starts_with(action: Str, file: Str, from_line: Int, line: Int, col: Int, prefix: Str) -> Bool {
    for d in diag_entries {
        if d.fix_action == action && d.file == file && d.fix_line >= from_line && d.fix_text.starts_with(prefix) {
            if d.fix_line < line || (d.fix_line == line && d.fix_col < col) { return true }
        }
    }
    false
}

// Drops the fix of entry `idx`, when it is `action`, if another error, or another fix, starts
// inside the text that fix replaces. The fix would rewrite code that is still wrong, or overlap
// the other fix, and `blink fix` applies neither of two overlapping fixes, so the inner one could
// never go first. Only a fix that rewrites the whole statement is at risk: a `let mut` fix
// replaces the `let` keyword alone, and an error reported at that `let` is about its value.
pub fn diag_drop_fix_over_others(idx: Int, action: Str) {
    if idx < 0 || idx >= diag_entries.len() { return }
    let d = diag_entries.get(idx).unwrap()
    if d.fix_action != action || d.fix_line == 0 { return }
    let mut i = 0
    while i < diag_entries.len() {
        let e = diag_entries.get(i).unwrap()
        let counts = e.severity == "error" || e.severity == "ice" || e.fix_action != ""
        let after_start = e.line > d.fix_line || (e.line == d.fix_line && e.col >= d.fix_col)
        let before_end = e.line < d.fix_end_line || (e.line == d.fix_end_line && e.col < d.fix_end_col)
        if i != idx && counts && e.file == d.file && after_start && before_end {
            diag_entries.set(idx, DiagEntry { fix_action: "", fix_text: "", fix_line: 0, fix_col: 0, fix_end_line: 0, fix_end_col: 0, ..d })
            return
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// The errors with this code among the entries after the first `n`.
pub fn diag_errors_since(n: Int, code: Str) -> Int {
    let mut count = 0
    let mut i = n
    while i < diag_entries.len() {
        let d = diag_entries.get(i).unwrap()
        if d.severity == "error" && d.code == code { count = count + 1 }
        i = i + 1
    }
    count
}

// The errors among the entries after the first `n`, whatever their code. A warning is not
// one, so a caller that asks whether a read went clean is not turned back by a lint.
pub fn diag_any_errors_since(n: Int) -> Int {
    let mut count = 0
    let mut i = n
    while i < diag_entries.len() {
        if diag_entries.get(i).unwrap().severity == "error" { count = count + 1 }
        i = i + 1
    }
    count
}

// Whether an error with this code is already recorded at column `col` of the node's line.
pub fn diag_has_error_at(code: Str, node_id: Int, col: Int) -> Bool {
    let file = diag_file_for_node(node_id)
    let line = node_line(node_id)
    for d in diag_entries {
        if d.severity == "error" && d.code == code && d.line == line && d.col == col && d.file == file { return true }
    }
    false
}

// The errors recorded in `file` on lines `line` through `end_line`, whatever their code.
pub fn diag_errors_in_lines(file: Str, line: Int, end_line: Int) -> Int {
    let mut count = 0
    for d in diag_entries {
        if d.severity == "error" && d.file == file && d.line >= line && d.line <= end_line { count = count + 1 }
    }
    count
}

// Roll back every diagnostic emitted after the first `n` entries. Used by
// the parser to collapse an error cascade (e.g. an unparseable string
// interpolation) into a single focused diagnostic.
// An internal error counts per report and renders once, so rolling back its entry gives
// back one count and leaves any suppressed repeat counted. The parser collapses parse
// cascades, which are errors, so nothing rolls back an internal error today.
pub fn diag_truncate(n: Int) {
    if diag_filter.flushed > n { diag_filter.flushed = n }
    while diag_entries.len() > n {
        let d = diag_entries.pop().unwrap()
        // Drop the dedup key too, so diag_filter.seen mirrors diag_entries —
        // a popped cascade entry must not suppress a later distinct re-emission.
        let dkey = "{d.severity}|{d.code}|{d.file}|{d.line}|{d.col}|{d.message}"
        diag_filter.seen.remove(dkey)
        if d.severity == "error" {
            diag_count = diag_count - 1
        } else if d.severity == "warning" {
            diag_warn_count = diag_warn_count - 1
        } else if d.severity == "ice" {
            diag_ice_count = diag_ice_count - 1
            diag_ice_state.rendered = diag_ice_state.rendered - 1
        }
    }
}

pub fn diag_error(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, line: Int, col: Int, help: Str) ! Diag.Report {
    diag_emit("error", name, code, message, line, col, help)
}

pub fn diag_error_range(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, line: Int, col: Int, end_col: Int, help: Str) ! Diag.Report {
    diag_emit_range("error", name, code, message, line, col, 0, end_col, help)
}

// An error whose span may end on a later line than it starts.
pub fn diag_error_span(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, line: Int, col: Int, end_line: Int, end_col: Int, help: Str) ! Diag.Report {
    diag_emit_range("error", name, code, message, line, col, end_line, end_col, help)
}

// An error whose span ends on its own line, so its row carries end_line and end_col.
pub fn diag_error_span_notes(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, line: Int, col: Int, end_col: Int, help: Str, notes: List[Str]) ! Diag.Report {
    diag_emit_range_notes("error", name, code, message, line, col, line, end_col, help, notes)
}

// From here on diag_record drops what the other stages report in diag_source_file at or after
// line:col, and every warning in that file. The lexer calls it for a literal open to the end
// of the file: the later stages saw the rest of the file as that literal's text, so an error
// past its start, or an unused binding whose uses it swallowed, would report the same mistake
// a second time.
pub fn diag_cut_after(line: Int, col: Int) {
    diag_filter.lex_cutoffs.push(LexCutoff { file: diag_source_file, line: line, col: col })
}

pub fn diag_cutoff_count() -> Int {
    diag_filter.lex_cutoffs.len()
}

fn diag_past_cutoff(severity: Str, code: Str, line: Int, col: Int) -> Bool {
    if diag_is_lexer_code(code) { return false }
    for c in diag_filter.lex_cutoffs {
        if c.file == diag_source_file {
            if severity == "warning" { return true }
            if line > c.line || (line == c.line && col >= c.col) { return true }
        }
    }
    false
}

pub fn diag_error_no_loc(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, help: Str) ! Diag.Report {
    diag_emit("error", name, code, message, 0, 0, help)
}

pub fn diag_error_no_loc_notes(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, help: Str, notes: List[Str]) ! Diag.Report {
    diag_emit_range_notes("error", name, code, message, 0, 0, 0, 0, help, notes)
}

// The file a node was parsed from. The parse ranges come first: a module may span files
// (`@module`), and its module entry names only the last of them.
pub fn diag_file_for_node(node_id: Int) -> Str {
    let mut ri = 0
    while ri < file_ranges.len() {
        let fr = file_ranges.get(ri).unwrap()
        if node_id >= fr.start && node_id < fr.end {
            return fr.path
        }
        ri = ri + 1
    }
    let mod_name = node_module(node_id)
    if mod_name != "" {
        let f = diag_module_files.get(mod_name) ?? ""
        if f != "" {
            return f
        }
    }
    diag_source_file
}

@allow(UnrestoredMutation, IncompleteStateRestore)
pub fn diag_error_at(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, node_id: Int, help: Str) ! Diag.Report {
    let line = node_line(node_id)
    let col = node_col(node_id)
    let saved_file = diag_source_file
    diag_source_file = diag_file_for_node(node_id)
    diag_emit("error", name, code, message, line, col, help)
    diag_source_file = saved_file
}

fn diag_warn(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, line: Int, col: Int, help: Str) ! Diag.Report {
    diag_emit("warning", name, code, message, line, col, help)
}

fn diag_warn_no_loc(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, help: Str) ! Diag.Report {
    diag_emit("warning", name, code, message, 0, 0, help)
}

@allow(UnrestoredMutation, IncompleteStateRestore)
pub fn diag_warn_at(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, node_id: Int, help: Str) ! Diag.Report {
    let line = node_line(node_id)
    let col = node_col(node_id)
    let saved_file = diag_source_file
    diag_source_file = diag_file_for_node(node_id)
    diag_emit("warning", name, code, message, line, col, help)
    diag_source_file = saved_file
}

@allow(UnrestoredMutation, IncompleteStateRestore)
pub fn diag_warn_at_notes(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, node_id: Int, help: Str, notes: List[Str]) ! Diag.Report {
    let line = node_line(node_id)
    let col = node_col(node_id)
    let saved_file = diag_source_file
    diag_source_file = diag_file_for_node(node_id)
    diag_emit_range_notes("warning", name, code, message, line, col, 0, 0, help, notes)
    diag_source_file = saved_file
}

// A warning whose caret runs from the node's start to `end_col` on the same line.
@allow(UnrestoredMutation, IncompleteStateRestore)
pub fn diag_warn_span_at_notes(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, node_id: Int, end_col: Int, help: Str, notes: List[Str]) ! Diag.Report {
    let line = node_line(node_id)
    let col = node_col(node_id)
    let saved_file = diag_source_file
    diag_source_file = diag_file_for_node(node_id)
    diag_emit_range_notes("warning", name, code, message, line, col, line, end_col, help, notes)
    diag_source_file = saved_file
}

// diag_warn_span_at_notes with a label after the carets.
pub fn diag_warn_span_label_at_notes(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, node_id: Int, end_col: Int, label: Str, help: Str, notes: List[Str]) ! Diag.Report {
    let before = diag_entries.len()
    diag_warn_span_at_notes(name, code, message, node_id, end_col, help, notes)
    diag_label_last_since(before, label)
}

// diag_warn_at_notes with a label after the caret.
pub fn diag_warn_label_at_notes(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, node_id: Int, label: Str, help: Str, notes: List[Str]) ! Diag.Report {
    let before = diag_entries.len()
    diag_warn_at_notes(name, code, message, node_id, help, notes)
    diag_label_last_since(before, label)
}

// diag_error_at_notes with a label after the carets.
pub fn diag_error_label_at_notes(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, node_id: Int, label: Str, help: Str, notes: List[Str]) ! Diag.Report {
    let before = diag_entries.len()
    diag_error_at_notes(name, code, message, node_id, help, notes)
    diag_label_last_since(before, label)
}

// diag_error_span_at_notes with a label after the carets.
pub fn diag_error_span_label_at_notes(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, node_id: Int, line: Int, col: Int, end_col: Int, label: Str, help: Str, notes: List[Str]) ! Diag.Report {
    let before = diag_entries.len()
    diag_error_span_at_notes(name, code, message, node_id, line, col, end_col, help, notes)
    diag_label_last_since(before, label)
}

// Puts `label` on the newest entry only when one was recorded after `before`: an `off` level
// or a repeat records nothing, and the last entry then belongs to another diagnostic.
fn diag_label_last_since(before: Int, label: Str) ! Diag.Report {
    if diag_entries.len() <= before { return }
    let idx = diag_entries.len() - 1
    let d = diag_entries.get(idx).unwrap()
    diag_entries.set(idx, DiagEntry { label: label, ..d })
}

// A warning about a whole file rather than a node in it: module resolution reports one
// before the file is parsed, so no node id exists yet.
@allow(UnrestoredMutation, IncompleteStateRestore)
pub fn diag_warn_in_file_notes(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, file: Str, line: Int, col: Int, help: Str, notes: List[Str]) ! Diag.Report {
    let saved_file = diag_source_file
    diag_source_file = file
    diag_emit_range_notes("warning", name, code, message, line, col, 0, 0, help, notes)
    diag_source_file = saved_file
}

// A diagnostic in a file the compiler reads but does not parse as Blink, such as blink.toml,
// at a span the caller read from that file.
@allow(UnrestoredMutation, IncompleteStateRestore)
pub fn diag_emit_in_file(severity: Str, name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, file: Str, line: Int, col: Int, end_line: Int, end_col: Int, help: Str) ! Diag.Report {
    let saved_file = diag_source_file
    diag_source_file = file
    diag_emit_range_notes(severity, name, code, message, line, col, end_line, end_col, help, [])
    diag_source_file = saved_file
}

// Error with secondary blame span(s) carried in `notes`: the primary caret sits
// at `node_id` (the binding), and each note points the reader at a
// contributing sub-expression (e.g. the empty `Map()` whose element is open).
pub fn diag_error_at_notes(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, node_id: Int, help: Str, notes: List[Str]) ! Diag.Report {
    diag_error_span_at_notes(name, code, message, node_id, node_line(node_id), node_col(node_id), 0, help, notes)
}

// An error with its caret on one line of the node's file, from `col` up to `end_col`
// (0: the default caret). The caller finds the place when the node's own is not where the
// caret belongs: a declaration starts at its keyword, and the error is about its name.
@allow(UnrestoredMutation, IncompleteStateRestore)
pub fn diag_error_span_at_notes(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, node_id: Int, line: Int, col: Int, end_col: Int, help: Str, notes: List[Str]) ! Diag.Report {
    let saved_file = diag_source_file
    diag_source_file = diag_file_for_node(node_id)
    let end_line = if end_col > 0 { line } else { 0 }
    diag_emit_range_notes("error", name, code, message, line, col, end_line, end_col, help, notes)
    diag_source_file = saved_file
}

pub fn diag_ice(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, line: Int, col: Int, help: Str) ! Diag.Report {
    diag_emit("ice", name, code, message, line, col, help)
}

// An internal error for a stage whose own state can not hold what comes next, so it can not
// go on after it as it does after diag_ice. It records the fault as diag_ice does, prints
// all that was reported in the run's format, and exits 101 as any internal error does.
pub fn diag_ice_abort(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str) {
    diag_record("ice", name, code, message, 0, 0, 0, 0, "", [])
    io.eprint(diag_unflushed(diag_format))
    io.print(diag_ice_state.abort_stdout)
    if diag_ice_state.abort_lsp_uri != "" {
        io.print(lsp_abort_frames(diag_ice_state.abort_lsp_uri, diag_ice_state.abort_lsp_request, name, code, message))
    }
    env.exit(101)
}

// An internal error is raised when a stage's own invariants have already failed, so the
// node id it carries comes out of that same broken state and is the one argument least
// able to be trusted. Reading a span from an id the pool cannot index ends the process
// through the accessor's own bounds panic, and the fault the report exists to state is
// never written down. A span is the optional part: without a readable id the report goes
// out with none.
fn diag_span_readable(node_id: Int) -> Bool {
    node_id >= 0 && node_id < node_count()
}

@allow(UnrestoredMutation, IncompleteStateRestore)
pub fn diag_ice_at(name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, node_id: Int, help: Str) ! Diag.Report {
    if !diag_span_readable(node_id) {
        diag_emit("ice", name, code, message, 0, 0, help)
        return
    }
    let line = node_line(node_id)
    let col = node_col(node_id)
    let saved_file = diag_source_file
    diag_source_file = diag_file_for_node(node_id)
    diag_emit("ice", name, code, message, line, col, help)
    diag_source_file = saved_file
}

// Every unbuilt code-generation stage reports the same name, code and help, so the
// triple lives here once instead of drifting across the stage files. Like every other
// diagnostic this RECORDS and returns; it does not unwind. The driver must read
// diag_ice_count before it uses what the stage handed back.
pub fn diag_ice_stage_not_built(stage: Str, node: Int) ! Diag.Report {
    diag_ice_at(CODEGEN_STAGE_NOT_BUILT_NAME, CODEGEN_STAGE_NOT_BUILT,
        "{stage} is not built in this compiler", node,
        "the tid-native emitters land stage by stage; use a released compiler build")
}

fn diag_emit_range(severity: Str, name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, line: Int, col: Int, end_line: Int, end_col: Int, help: Str) ! Diag.Report {
    diag_emit_range_notes(severity, name, code, message, line, col, end_line, end_col, help, [])
}

fn diag_emit_range_notes(severity: Str, name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, line: Int, col: Int, end_line: Int, end_col: Int, help: Str, notes: List[Str]) ! Diag.Report {
    let effective_severity = if severity == "ice" { severity } else { lint_apply_override(severity, name, code) }
    diag_record(effective_severity, name, code, message, line, col, end_line, end_col, help, notes)
}

// The one place a diagnostic is recorded, with its lint override already applied. It has no
// Diag.Report so that diag_ice_abort can record the fault from a stage, such as the parser's
// node pool, that declares no effect: that call never returns, so no caller is left to see
// the report.
fn diag_record(effective_severity: Str, name: Str, code: Str, message: Str, line: Int, col: Int, end_line: Int, end_col: Int, help: Str, notes: List[Str]) {
    if effective_severity == "off" {
        return
    }
    // An internal error is a fault in the compiler, never a follow-on, so it is always shown.
    if effective_severity != "ice" && diag_filter.lex_cutoffs.len() > 0 && diag_past_cutoff(effective_severity, code, line, col) {
        return
    }
    let dkey = "{effective_severity}|{code}|{diag_source_file}|{line}|{col}|{message}"
    // An internal error counts every time it is reported, before the repeat is dropped.
    // A caller reads the count to decide whether what a stage handed back is usable, and
    // two emits of one program raise the same fault at the same place, so a count of
    // distinct reports goes flat on the second and the caller accepts a unit with a body
    // missing. Suppressing the repeat stays a rendering rule: the reader still sees it once.
    if effective_severity == "ice" { diag_ice_count = diag_ice_count + 1 }
    if diag_filter.seen.contains_key(dkey) != 0 {
        return
    }
    diag_filter.seen.insert(dkey, 1)
    diag_entries.push(DiagEntry {
        severity: effective_severity, name: name, code: code,
        message: message, file: diag_source_file, line: line,
        col: col, help: help, end_line: end_line, end_col: end_col,
        fix_action: "", fix_text: "", fix_line: 0, fix_col: 0, fix_end_line: 0, fix_end_col: 0,
        notes: notes
    })
    if effective_severity == "error" {
        diag_count = diag_count + 1
    } else if effective_severity == "warning" {
        diag_warn_count = diag_warn_count + 1
    } else if effective_severity == "ice" {
        diag_ice_state.rendered = diag_ice_state.rendered + 1
    }
}

pub fn diag_ice_rendered_count() -> Int { diag_ice_state.rendered }

// ── JSON string escaping ─────────────────────────────────────────────

// JSON allows no raw byte below 0x20 in a string, so each one the short escapes miss is
// written as `\u00XX`.
pub fn json_escape(s: Str) -> Str {
    let mut sb = StringBuilder.new()
    // Unescaped bytes are copied a run at a time: a run ends only at an ASCII byte, so it
    // never cuts a multi-byte codepoint.
    let mut run = 0
    let mut i = 0
    while i < s.byte_len() {
        let ch = s.byte_at(i)
        let esc = if ch == 92 {
            "\\\\"
        } else if ch == 34 {
            "\\\""
        } else if ch == 10 {
            "\\n"
        } else if ch == 13 {
            "\\r"
        } else if ch == 9 {
            "\\t"
        } else if ch < 32 {
            let hex = "0123456789abcdef"
            let n = ch.to_int()
            "\\u00{hex.byte_slice(n / 16, n / 16 + 1)}{hex.byte_slice(n % 16, n % 16 + 1)}"
        } else {
            ""
        }
        if esc != "" {
            sb.write(s.byte_slice(run, i))
            sb.write(esc)
            run = i + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    sb.write(s.byte_slice(run, s.byte_len()))
    sb.to_str()
}

// ── Flush: emit all diagnostics ──────────────────────────────────────

pub fn diag_flush() ! Diag.Report {
    let text = diag_unflushed(diag_format)
    diag_filter.flushed = diag_entries.len()
    io.eprint(text)
}

// Marks the first n entries printed, for a caller that printed them as rendered text.
pub fn diag_mark_flushed(n: Int) {
    diag_filter.flushed = n
}

// What the next diag_flush prints. `format` is 1 for JSON lines, 0 for the human form.
pub fn diag_unflushed(format: Int) -> Str {
    diag_render_from(diag_filter.flushed, format)
}

// Every entry as diag_flush prints it, as text: the daemon renders a check for a client to
// print, and the two must give the same bytes. `format` is 1 for JSON lines, 0 for the human
// form.
pub fn diag_render(format: Int) -> Str {
    diag_render_from(0, format)
}

// The entries from index `from` on, and the summary when there are any.
fn diag_render_from(from: Int, format: Int) -> Str {
    if from >= diag_entries.len() {
        return ""
    }
    let mut sb = StringBuilder.new()
    let mut i = from
    while i < diag_entries.len() {
        if format == 1 {
            sb.write(diag_json_line(i))
        } else {
            sb.write(diag_human_text(i))
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if format != 1 {
        // The summary counts what was printed, not what was reported: a repeat is dropped
        // from the listing above, so counting reports here would name a number the reader
        // can not find.
        if diag_ice_state.rendered > 0 {
            sb.write("{diag_ice_state.rendered} internal compiler error(s)\n")
        }
        if diag_count > 0 {
            sb.write("{diag_count} error(s) found\n")
        }
    }
    sb.to_str()
}

// The entry at idx as diag_flush prints it, without the summary: a command that prints only
// some entries prints them as `check` would. `format` is 1 for a JSON line, 0 for the human form.
pub fn diag_entry_text(idx: Int, format: Int) -> Str {
    if format == 1 { diag_json_line(idx) } else { diag_human_text(idx) }
}

fn diag_json_line(idx: Int) -> Str {
    let d = diag_entries.get(idx).unwrap()
    let sev = json_escape(d.severity)
    let name = json_escape(d.name)
    let code = json_escape(d.code)
    let msg = json_escape(d.message)
    let file = json_escape(d.file)
    let line = d.line
    let col = d.col
    let help = d.help
    let el = d.end_line
    let ec = d.end_col
    let pos = if el > 0 { "\"line\":{line},\"col\":{col},\"end_line\":{el},\"end_col\":{ec}" } else { "\"line\":{line},\"col\":{col}" }
    let span = "\"span\":\{\"file\":\"{file}\",{pos}}"
    let mut json = "\{\"severity\":\"{sev}\",\"name\":\"{name}\",\"code\":\"{code}\",\"message\":\"{msg}\",{span}"
    // The schema of sections/06_tooling.md §8.6 lists labels with their own spans; the one
    // label an entry holds marks its primary span.
    if d.label != "" {
        json = json.concat(",\"labels\":[\{\"span\":\{{pos}},\"message\":\"").concat(json_escape(d.label)).concat("\"}]")
    }
    if help != "" {
        json = json.concat(",\"help\":\"").concat(json_escape(help)).concat("\"")
    }
    if d.notes.len() > 0 {
        json = json.concat(",\"notes\":[")
        let mut ni = 0
        while ni < d.notes.len() {
            if ni > 0 { json = json.concat(",") }
            json = json.concat("\"").concat(json_escape(d.notes.get(ni).unwrap())).concat("\"")
            ni = ni + 1
        }
        json = json.concat("]")
    }
    let fix_act = d.fix_action
    if fix_act != "" {
        let fix_txt = json_escape(d.fix_text)
        let mut fix_span = ""
        if d.fix_line > 0 {
            fix_span = ",\"span\":\{\"file\":\"{file}\",\"line\":{d.fix_line},\"col\":{d.fix_col},\"end_line\":{d.fix_end_line},\"end_col\":{d.fix_end_col}}"
        }
        json = json.concat(",\"fix\":\{\"action\":\"{fix_act}\",\"text\":\"{fix_txt}\"{fix_span}}")
    }
    // An assist is a repair a person must finish or choose, so it never shares the `fix` key
    // that `blink fix` applies.
    if d.assist_action != "" {
        let as_txt = json_escape(d.assist_text)
        json = json.concat(",\"assist\":\{\"action\":\"{d.assist_action}\",\"text\":\"{as_txt}\",\"span\":\{\"file\":\"{file}\",\"line\":{d.assist_line},\"col\":{d.assist_col}}}")
    }
    json.concat("}\n")
}

// One line of a source file as written, or "" when it cannot be read, so a help can quote the
// user's own code.
pub fn diag_source_line(file: Str, line: Int) -> Str {
    source_get_line(file, line)
}

fn source_get_line(file: Str, line: Int) -> Str {
    if file == "" {
        return ""
    }
    if source_lines_cache.contains_key(file) == false {
        if file_exists(file) == 0 {
            return ""
        }
        source_lines_cache.insert(file, read_file(file).split("\n"))
    }
    let lines = source_lines_cache.get(file).unwrap()
    let idx = line - 1
    if idx < 0 || idx >= lines.len() {
        return ""
    }
    lines.get(idx).unwrap()
}

fn str_repeat(s: Str, n: Int) -> Str {
    let mut sb = StringBuilder.with_capacity(if n > 0 { n * s.byte_len() } else { 0 })
    let mut i = 0
    while i < n {
        sb.write(s)
        i = i + 1
    }
    sb.to_str()
}

fn int_digit_count(n: Int) -> Int {
    if n < 10 { return 1 }
    if n < 100 { return 2 }
    if n < 1000 { return 3 }
    if n < 10000 { return 4 }
    5
}

// A help carrying newlines is several `help:` lines, not one line with newlines in it. A
// repair sometimes needs a second, independent suggestion (sections/07 §9.1.1 requires one),
// and an entry holds a single help field.
fn diag_help_text(help: Str) -> Str {
    let mut sb = StringBuilder.new()
    let mut seg_start = 0
    let mut i = 0
    while i <= help.byte_len() {
        if i == help.byte_len() || help.byte_at(i) == 10 {
            if i > seg_start {
                sb.write("  help: {help.byte_slice(seg_start, i)}\n")
            }
            seg_start = i + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    sb.to_str()
}

fn diag_human_text(idx: Int) -> Str {
    let d = diag_entries.get(idx).unwrap()
    let mut sb = StringBuilder.new()
    let display_severity = if d.severity == "ice" { "internal compiler error" } else { d.severity }
    sb.write("{display_severity}[{d.name}]: {d.message}\n")
    if d.line > 0 {
        sb.write("  --> {d.file}:{d.line}:{d.col}\n")
        let src_line = source_get_line(d.file, d.line)
        if src_line != "" {
            let gutter_w = int_digit_count(d.line) + 1
            let gutter_pad = str_repeat(" ", gutter_w)
            sb.write("{gutter_pad}|\n")
            sb.write("{d.line} | {src_line}\n")
            let caret_len = if d.end_col > d.col { d.end_col - d.col } else { 1 }
            let caret_col = if d.col > 1 { d.col - 1 } else { 0 }
            let caret_pad = str_repeat(" ", caret_col)
            let label = if d.label != "" { " {d.label}" } else { "" }
            sb.write("{gutter_pad}| {caret_pad}{str_repeat("^", caret_len)}{label}\n")
        }
    }
    let mut ni = 0
    while ni < d.notes.len() {
        sb.write("  note: {d.notes.get(ni).unwrap()}\n")
        ni = ni + 1
    }
    if d.severity == "ice" {
        if d.help != "" {
            sb.write(diag_help_text(d.help))
        }
        sb.write("  note: This is a compiler bug. Please report it at https://github.com/blinklang/blink/issues\n")
    } else if d.help != "" {
        sb.write(diag_help_text(d.help))
    }
    if d.fix_action != "" && d.fix_line > 0 {
        sb.write("  fix: {d.fix_action} `{d.fix_text}` at {d.fix_line}:{d.fix_col}\n")
    } else if d.fix_action != "" {
        sb.write("  fix: {d.fix_action} `{d.fix_text}`\n")
    }
    sb.to_str()
}

// Puts entries that earlier compiles of the same command reported before the current ones.
// Each compile resets the sink, so a command that compiles several files and flushes once
// keeps them this way. A repeat is dropped, as diag_record drops one: a module that two of
// the files import reports its warning once.
pub fn diag_prepend(entries: List[DiagEntry]) {
    let mut out: List[DiagEntry] = []
    for d in entries {
        let dkey = "{d.severity}|{d.code}|{d.file}|{d.line}|{d.col}|{d.message}"
        if diag_filter.seen.contains_key(dkey) == 0 {
            diag_filter.seen.insert(dkey, 1)
            out.push(d)
            if d.severity == "error" {
                diag_count = diag_count + 1
            } else if d.severity == "warning" {
                diag_warn_count = diag_warn_count + 1
            } else if d.severity == "ice" {
                diag_ice_count = diag_ice_count + 1
                diag_ice_state.rendered = diag_ice_state.rendered + 1
            }
        }
    }
    for d in diag_entries {
        out.push(d)
    }
    diag_entries = out
}

// ── Reset ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

pub fn diag_reset() {
    diag_entries.clear()
    diag_count = 0
    diag_warn_count = 0
    diag_ice_count = 0
    // The abort output belongs to the command, which sets it before the compile that resets here.
    diag_ice_state = DiagIceState {
        rendered: 0, abort_stdout: diag_ice_state.abort_stdout,
        abort_lsp_uri: diag_ice_state.abort_lsp_uri, abort_lsp_request: diag_ice_state.abort_lsp_request,
    }
    diag_module_files.clear()
    file_ranges.clear()
    lint_overrides.clear()
    diag_allow_set.clear()
    diag_allow_stack.clear()
    source_lines_cache.clear()
    diag_filter = DiagFilter { seen: Map(), lex_cutoffs: [], flushed: 0 }
}

// ── Error catalog ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

pub fn diag_explain(code: Str) -> Str {
    if code == NON_EXHAUSTIVE_MATCH {
        return "E0004 -- NonExhaustiveMatch\n\nEvery `match` must cover all possible values of its scrutinee. This\none leaves at least one enum variant unhandled, and the compiler\nrejects it rather than letting the unmatched value fall through.\n\nFalling through is not a harmless default. In expression position\nthere is no value to produce, so an unhandled variant would read an\nuninitialized slot; in statement position it would silently skip the\nmatch. Neither is what the code says.\n\n  type Color \{ Red, Green, Blue \}\n\n  match c \{                 // E0004 -- missing pattern: `Blue`\n      Color.Red => \"r\"\n      Color.Green => \"g\"\n  \}\n\nFix: add an arm for each variant the message names.\n\n  match c \{                 // OK -- every variant covered\n      Color.Red => \"r\"\n      Color.Green => \"g\"\n      Color.Blue => \"b\"\n  \}\n\nA wildcard `_` arm, or a plain binding arm, also makes the match\nexhaustive -- but it silently absorbs variants added later, so prefer\nnaming variants when you want the compiler to find those sites.\n\n  match c \{\n      Color.Red => \"r\"\n      _ => \"other\"          // OK -- catch-all\n  \}\n\nA guard does NOT contribute to exhaustiveness: `Color.Blue if f` may\nbe false, so `Blue` still counts as unhandled."
    }
    if code == INCONSISTENT_PATTERN_BINDINGS {
        return "E0005 -- InconsistentPatternBindings\n\nThe alternatives of an OR-pattern share one arm body, so each name the\nbody reads must come from whichever alternative matched. Every\nalternative must bind the same names, and each name must have the same\ntype and the same `mut` in every alternative.\n\n  match o \{\n      Some(x) | None => use(x)      // E0005 -- `None` does not bind `x`\n  \}\n\n  match r \{                         // r: Result[Int, Str]\n      Ok(x) | Err(x) => show(x)     // E0005 -- `x` is `Int`, then `Str`\n  \}\n\n  match e \{\n      Click(mut x) | Touch(x) => step(x)   // E0005 -- `x` is `mut` in one\n  \}\n\nFix: bind the name in every alternative at one type, with `mut` in\nevery alternative or in none, or split the alternatives into arms of\ntheir own.\n\n  match o \{\n      Some(x) => use(x)\n      None => use(0)\n  \}"
    }
    if code == TYPE_ERROR {
        return "E0300 -- TypeError\n\nA type mismatch was detected during type checking.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Passing a value of the wrong type to a function\n  - Using `?` in a function that does not return Result\n  - Assigning a value to a variable of incompatible type\n\nFix: check the expected and actual types in the error message and\nadjust your code to match.\n\n  fn add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int \{ a + b \}\n  add(1, \"two\")  // E0300: expected Int, got Str"
    }
    if code == CANNOT_INFER_TYPE {
        return "E0301 -- CannotInferType\n\nEvery binding must resolve to a concrete type. This one is\nunder-determined: an empty container or bare None was never given\nan element type -- not by an annotation, and not by a later use\nthat would constrain it. There is no default type; the compiler\nreports it instead of guessing.\n\nA constraining use DOES fix it: pushing/inserting an element, or\npassing the value where a concrete type is expected.\n\n  let mut xs = []\n  xs.push(1)              // OK -- later use infers List[Int]\n\n  let x = []\n  let n = x.len()         // E0301 -- len() does not constrain the element\n\nFix: add a type annotation.\n\n  let x: List[Int] = []   // OK\n  let m: Map[Str, Int] = Map()\n  let o: Int? = None"
    }
    if code == NOT_ITERABLE {
        return "E0302 -- NotIterable\n\nA `for` loop's iterable is not one of Blink's built-in iterable\ntypes. Only these implement IntoIterator:\n\n  List[T]    -- yields T, in order\n  Set[T]     -- yields T, unspecified order\n  Map[K, V]  -- yields (K, V), unspecified order\n  Range[T]   -- yields T, lazy\n  Str        -- yields Char\n  Bytes      -- yields U8\n  Channel[T] -- yields T (receive)\n  Iterator[T] -- yields T (identity)\n\nFix: iterate one of the types above, or call `.into_iter()` /\n`.iter()` on a type that provides one.\n\n  for x in 5 \{ }              // E0302 -- Int is not iterable\n  for x in (List[Int]) \{ }    // OK"
    }
    if code == TYPE_ARG_ARITY {
        return "E0303 -- TypeArgArity\n\nA type constructor named in a type position must be applied to\nexactly its declared number of type arguments. A constructor of\narity n denotes a complete type only when it is given n arguments;\nwriting the wrong number -- including none -- does not name a type.\n\nUnder-application (too few, of which a bare name is the extreme) and\nover-application (too many) are the same failure and the same code.\n\n  fn relay(src: Channel, dst: Channel) \{ }   // E0303 -- Channel takes 1, 0 given\n  fn f(m: Map[Str]) \{ }                       // E0303 -- Map takes 2, 1 given\n  fn g(xs: List[Int, Str]) \{ }                // E0303 -- List takes 1, 2 given\n\nThe rule is uniform across builtins (Channel, List, Map, Set,\nOption, Result, Iterator, Handle), user generics, and 0-arity\nscalars (Int, Str, ...): a bare `Registry` is E0303 for the same\nreason a bare `Channel` is, and `Int[Str]` for the same reason\n`List[Int, Str]` is. It is decided at name\nresolution, from the declaration alone, before inference runs -- so\nit never co-fires with error[CannotInferType] (E0301) on the same\ntype expression.\n\nA trait name in a bound (`T: Ord`) or an impl header is NOT a type\nposition; its arguments are governed by error[TraitArgArity]\n(E0910), not this rule. An impl-header RECEIVER, however, is a type\nposition: `impl Show for Box` is E0303.\n\nFix: supply exactly the arguments the constructor declares.\n\n  fn relay[T](src: Channel[T], dst: Channel[T]) \{ }   // OK\n  fn f[K, V](m: Map[K, V]) \{ }                         // OK\n  impl[T] Show for Box[T] \{ ... }                      // OK"
    }
    if code == MISSING_ELSE {
        return "E0304 -- MissingElse\n\nAn `if` used as a VALUE must have an `else`. In expression context --\na let binding, a return, or an argument -- the `if` has to produce a\nvalue on every path, so both branches are required and must unify to\nthe same type. A missing `else` has no value when the condition is\nfalse, so the compiler rejects it rather than reading an\nuninitialized slot.\n\n  let x = if c \{ 1 }            // E0304 -- no value when c is false\n  let x = if c \{ 1 } else \{ 0 } // OK\n\nIn STATEMENT context the value is discarded, so `else` is optional and\nthe `if` has type ():\n\n  if c \{ log(\"hi\") }            // OK -- value discarded\n\nFix: add an `else` branch, or -- if you meant to run the branch for its\neffect only -- make the `if` a statement rather than binding its value."
    }
    if code == UNLICENSED_SELF_OP {
        return "E0305 -- UnlicensedSelfOperation\n\nInside a trait's default method body, `self` has the abstract type\n`Self`. `Self` is an implicit type parameter bounded by the trait's\nGUARANTEE SET: the methods the trait declares, plus every method of\neach trait named in its supertrait clause, transitively. An operator\n(which desugars to a trait method -- see Operator Desugaring) or a\nplain method call on a value of type `Self` is well-formed only when\nits backing trait is in the guarantee set. Any other operation is\nrejected at the trait DEFINITION, before any implementor exists.\n\n  trait Ranked: Ord \{                       // OK -- `<` -> Ord.cmp,\n      fn before(self, o: Self) -> Bool \{      //      Ord is a supertrait\n          self < o\n      \}\n  \}\n\n  trait Ranked \{                            // E0305 -- `<` needs Ord,\n      fn before(self, o: Self) -> Bool \{      //          not declared\n          self < o\n      \}\n  \}\n\nFix (open trait): declare the backing trait as a supertrait so every\nimplementor provides the operation -- `trait Ranked: Ord \{ ... \}`.\n\nThe arithmetic traits (Add, Sub, Mul, Div, Rem, Neg) are SEALED to the\nbuilt-in numeric types. A trait may still name one as a supertrait\n(`trait Doubler: Add`), but that restricts its implementors to the\nnumerics -- no user type can satisfy it -- so the diagnostic for a\nsealed backing trait does NOT suggest adding it. Make the method\nrequired instead, or use a concrete numeric type.\n\nA call to one of the trait's OWN methods is always licensed: that is\nwhat lets an `ne` default call `self.eq()`."
    }
    if code == TRAIT_BOUND_NOT_SATISFIED {
        return "E0306 -- TraitBoundNotSatisfied\n\nA type parameter may declare bounds -- `fn sum[T: Add](a: T, b: T)`\nsays every instantiation of `T` must implement Add. Bounds are\nchecked against the arguments AS WRITTEN. When a call's type argument\n(inferred from the arguments, or written explicitly) does not satisfy\nthe binder's bound, the call is rejected here -- at the call site,\nbefore code generation -- not left to fail later in the C backend.\nExplicitness never bypasses a bound.\n\n  fn sum[T: Add](a: T, b: T) -> T \{ a + b \}\n  sum(1, 2)        // OK   -- T := Int, and Int implements Add\n  sum(\"a\", \"b\")    // E0306 -- T := Str, and Str does not implement Add\n\nThe arithmetic traits (Add, Sub, Mul, Div, Rem, Neg) are SEALED to the\nbuilt-in numeric types, so a bounded `T` can only ever be a numeric\ntype -- passing a Str, Bool, or a user type is rejected. The modeled\ntraits also include Ord, Eq, Display, Debug, Hash, and Serialize.\n\nThe same code answers a qualified trait call inside a generic body.\n`Trait.method(v)` names ONE trait, and on a type-parameter receiver the\nbinder's bounds are the whole licence for the call: a qualifier the\nbounds do not name is rejected rather than dispatched to some other\ntrait that happens to define the method name.\n\n  fn f[T: A](v: T) -> Int \{ B.go(v) \}   // E0306 -- T is bound by A, not B\n  fn f[T: A + B](v: T) -> Int \{ B.go(v) \}  // OK\n\nFix: pass an argument whose type satisfies the bound, or -- for a\nuser type -- derive or implement the required trait for it. For the\nqualified-call form, add the named trait to the binder's bounds.\n\n  @derive(Ord) type Widget \{ x: Int \}   // now satisfies an Ord bound"
    }
    if code == CALL_SITE_TYPE_ARGS {
        return "E0307 -- CallSiteTypeArgs\n\nA call may write its type arguments in brackets before the argument\nlist, as in `probe[Int]()`. The list binds the callee's own type\nparameters in order, so it holds one type for each of them. This code\nrejects a list that cannot bind.\n\nThe count is wrong:\n\n  fn pair[A, B]() -> Int \{ 2 \}\n  let a = pair[Int]()          // E0307 -- pair declares 2, 1 given\n  let b = pair[Int, Str]()     // OK\n\nThe brackets have no call after them. Type arguments belong to a call,\nand a function named as a value has no call to take them:\n\n  let g = probe[Int]           // E0307\n  let v = probe[Int]()         // OK -- the call takes them\n\nThe callee takes no type arguments: a variant, a builtin, or a value.\nBrackets after a value are a type-argument list and never an index,\nbecause Blink has no index operator. To read an element, use\n`.get(...)` (§2.6):\n\n  let f = fns.get(i).unwrap()  // then call f()\n\nFix: write one type for each type parameter, or remove the brackets\nand let the arguments and the binding decide the types."
    }
    if code == ENUM_PATTERN_NON_ENUM {
        return "E0308 -- EnumPatternNeedsEnumScrutinee\n\nA match arm names a variant, but the type at that pattern position is\nnot an enum: a builtin scalar, a struct, a tuple, a list or other\nbuiltin type, or a type parameter. None of these has variants, so no\narm of that shape can ever be the one that runs.\n\nIn a match arm, a bare name whose first letter is uppercase names a\nvariant, with or without a payload. It never binds the value:\n\n  const MAX = 10\n\n  fn f(x: Int) -> Int \{\n      match x \{\n          MAX => 0                 // E0308 -- not a binding\n          n if n == MAX => 0       // OK: compare with a guard\n          other => other           // OK: a lowercase name binds\n      \}\n  \}\n\nA type parameter is reported where the generic function is defined.\n\n  type State \{ Idle, Running \}\n\n  fn name_of(n: Int) -> Str \{\n      match n \{\n          State.Idle => \"idle\"     // E0308 -- n is an Int\n          _ => \"other\"\n      \}\n  \}\n\nAn enum is a distinct type from Int. A variant lowers to an integer\ntag at runtime, but the tag's representation does not make the two\ntypes interchangeable, and the pattern side follows the same rule as\nthe assignability side.\n\nFix: match the enum itself, or cross the boundary explicitly.\nState.from_int(n) answers Option[State], because an arbitrary Int\nmay name no variant; State.to_int() goes the other way and is total.\n\n  match State.from_int(n) \{\n      Some(State.Idle) => \"idle\"   // OK\n      _ => \"other\"\n  \}\n\nComparison is unaffected. `n == State.Idle` compares the shared tag\nand stays legal; only match arms are governed by this rule."
    }
    if code == NO_CONVERSION_IMPL {
        return "E0312 -- NoConversionImpl\n\nA conversion names a source type and a target type, and no impl\ndeclares it. `x.into()` and `T.from(x)` need `impl From[S] for T`;\n`T.try_from(x)` needs `impl TryFrom[S] for T`. S is the type of x,\nand an alias counts as the type it names.\n\n  let f: Float = 2.5\n  let n: Int = f.into()        // E0312 -- no impl From[Float] for Int\n\nFrom is for conversions that cannot fail or lose data. Every type\nconverts to itself, and Int widens to Float. A narrowing or lossy\nconversion has a name of its own instead:\n\n  let n = f.truncate()               // Float to Int, drops the fraction\n  let w = i.to_float()               // Int to Float\n  let c = f.to_int_checked()         // Float to Int, fails out of range\n  let b = U8.try_from(i)             // Int to Result[U8, ConversionError]\n  let s = i.to_string()              // any Display value to Str\n\nFor your own types, declare the impl:\n\n  impl From[Int] for Meters \{\n      fn from(n: Int) -> Meters \{ Meters \{ v: n } }\n  }"
    }
    if code == NO_INDEX_OPERATOR {
        return "E0313 -- NoIndexOperator\n\nBlink has no index operator. Brackets after an expression hold\ntype arguments, so a value inside them is an error, whether the\nbrackets are read, written to, or called:\n\n  let x = xs[1]                // E0313\n  let v = fns[0](3)            // E0313\n  xs[0] = 5                    // E0313\n\nThe bracket contents decide it, at name resolution. A name in the\nbrackets is looked up as a type first; any item that is a value\n(a literal, a local, a const, an expression) makes this error.\n\nRead an element with `.get(...)`, which returns an Option, because\nthe position or key may be absent. A tuple field is `.0`, `.1`:\n\n  let x = xs.get(1)            // Option[Int]\n  let a = ages.get(\"bob\")     // Option[Int]\n  let t = pair.0\n\nWhen a call follows the brackets, `match` on the Option before you\ncall the element; no machine fix is offered.\n\nWrite an element with `.set(i, v)` on a List or Bytes, and with\n`.insert(k, v)` on a Map:\n\n  xs.set(0, 5)\n  ages.insert(\"bob\", 41)\n\nA compound store `xs[i] += 1` becomes\n`xs.set(i, xs.get(i).unwrap() + 1)`, which panics when `i` is out of\nbounds. That repair is offered as a fix only when the receiver is a plain\nbinding and the index is a literal, a local or a const; otherwise\nbind the index with `let` first."
    }
    if code == MISSING_FIELD {
        return "E0315 -- MissingField\n\nA struct literal, a variant brace literal or a variant paren payload\nthat writes a label left out a field that has no default\n(sections/02_syntax.md §2.14). The error sits at the type or variant\nname and lists each missing field with its type, in declaration order.\nIn a paren payload, argument i written by position fills field i, and\na note lists the fields in order and marks each filled one `(given)`.\nA literal with `..source` takes every field it does not label from the\nsource, so it never misses one.\n\n  type Point \{ x: Float, y: Float \}\n  let p = Point \{ x: 1.0 \}           // E0315 -- missing field `y` (Float)\n  let q = Point \{ x: 1.0, y: 2.0 \}   // OK\n  let r = Point \{ x: 3.0, ..q \}      // OK\n\n  type Figure \{ Circle(radius: Float, tag: Int) \}\n  let c = Figure.Circle(1.0, radius: 2.0)   // E0315 -- missing `tag` (Int)\n\nFix: give each missing field a value. For a struct declared in the\npackage being compiled, you may instead give the field a default in\nthe declaration. A variant field takes no default (§3.4). No machine\nfix is offered: only you know the value. An editor may offer to write\nthe missing labels for you to finish."
    }
    if code == PATTERN_MISSING_REST {
        return "E0319 -- PatternMissingRest\n\nA struct pattern or a variant brace pattern left out a field and has no\n`..` (sections/03_types.md §3.5). The error sits at the type or variant\nname and lists each left-out field with its type, in declaration\norder. A field default does not count as naming the field. A field\nthat is the one suggestion for an unknown label in the same pattern is\nnot left out: the rename fixes both.\n\n  type Point3 \{ x: Int, y: Int, z: Int \}\n  match p \{\n      Point3 \{ x, y \} => x        // E0319 -- leaves out `z` (Int)\n      Point3 \{ x, .. \} => x       // OK\n      Point3 \{ x, y, z: _ \} => x  // OK\n  \}\n\nFix: add `..` (a machine fix, and the preferred one), or write `f: _`\nfor each left-out field `f`. The second keeps the pattern closed, so a\nfield the type gains later is an error again; it is never applied for\nyou."
    }
    if code == MIXED_VARIANT_FIELDS {
        return "E0320 -- MixedVariantFields\n\nA paren variant names every field or none (sections/03_types.md §3.4).\nA variant that declares both an unnamed field and a named field, in\neither order, is an error at the variant. A label could not name the\nunnamed field, so no label rule would be complete for it. The variant\nis still declared, so its uses report nothing more.\n\n  type Shape \{\n      Circle(radius: Float, tag: Int)    // OK -- every field has a name\n      Pair(Int, Int)                     // OK -- no field has a name\n      Both(Int, b: Int)                  // E0320\n      Rev(a: Int, Int)                   // E0320\n  \}\n\nFix: name every field or none. No machine fix is offered, because only\nyou can choose the names."
    }
    if code == DUPLICATE_FIELD {
        return "E0316 -- DuplicateField\n\nOne literal, variant paren payload or brace pattern gave the same\nlabel twice (sections/02_syntax.md §2.14, sections/03_types.md §3.5).\nThe error sits at the second label, with a note at the first. A label\nbeside `..source` counts too: the source fills only the fields the\nliteral does not label. In a paren payload, argument i written by\nposition fills field i, so a label for that field is given twice; the\nnote is then at the argument.\n\n  let p = Point \{ x: 1.0, x: 2.0, y: 3.0 \}   // E0316 -- `x` is given twice\n  let c = Figure.Circle(1.0, radius: 2.0)     // E0316 -- argument 1 filled `radius`\n  match p \{ Point \{ x, x: z, .. \} => z \}      // E0316 -- `x` is given twice\n\nFix: remove one of the two. No machine fix is offered, because only\nyou know which value to keep."
    }
    if code == INT_LITERAL_OUT_OF_RANGE {
        return "E0318 -- IntLiteralOutOfRange\n\nAn integer literal is the exact value its digits spell, and the value\nmust be in the range of the literal's type (sections/03_types.md §3.2).\nThe type is the expected type at the literal, or Int when there is none.\nThe compiler never cuts a literal to fit a narrow type.\n\n  let a: U8 = 300           // E0318 -- 300 does not fit in U8 (0 to 255)\n  let b: I8 = -129          // E0318 -- -129 does not fit in I8 (-128 to 127)\n  let c = 9223372036854775808   // E0318 -- Int is the default type\n  let d: U64 = -1           // E0318 -- unsigned types hold no negative values\n\nThe expected type of a call reaches the type variables of its result\ntype before the arguments are checked. A literal checked against a type\nvariable that nothing has solved has no expected type, so it gets the\ndefault form, never a range. The help names the repair that reaches\nthe variable, and prints only when applying it compiles.\n\n  let w: U64 = id(9223372036854775808)   // OK -- U64 reaches T first\n  let y = id(9223372036854775808)        // E0318 -- help: let y = id[U64](...)\n  let o = Some(9223372036854775808)      // E0318 -- help: let o: Option[U64] = ...\n  let mut xs = []\n  xs.push(9223372036854775808)           // E0318 -- the element type is not known\n                                         //   help: let mut xs: List[U64] = []\n\nA `-` directly before a literal, with optional parentheses, makes one\nnegative literal: `-128`, `(-128)` and `-(128)` are the same value.\nUnder an unsigned type every negative literal is E0318, `-0` included.\nThe rule also covers literal patterns, range-pattern bounds and const\nvalues. No `@allow` turns it off.\n\nFix: give the value a type that holds it, or change the value.\n\n  let c: U64 = 9223372036854775808   // OK\n  let a: U16 = 300                   // OK"
    }
    if code == VARIANT_SPREAD {
        return "E0317 -- VariantSpread\n\nA variant brace literal held `..source`. Copy-update applies to struct\nliterals only (sections/02_syntax.md §2.16, sections/03_types.md §3.4):\na variant value may be another variant, so its fields cannot be copied\nwithout a match.\n\n  let r = Figure.Rect \{ width: 1.0, ..s \}    // E0317\n\nFix: write every field, or match on the source to bind its fields:\n\n  match s \{\n      Figure.Rect \{ width, height \} => Figure.Rect \{ width: 2.0, height: height \}\n      _ => s\n  \}"
    }
    if code == MISSING_RETURN {
        return "E0311 -- MissingReturn\n\nControl can reach the end of a block that must produce a value of\ntype R along a path that yields (). A value-returning function whose\ntail can complete normally falls off the end with no value, so the\ncompiler rejects it instead of fabricating one.\n\nA `while` or `for` always has type (): the condition may be false on\nentry, and the iterable may be empty or exhaust. `while true` is not\nspecial -- the checker does not fold the condition.\n\n  fn first(n: Int) -> Int \{        // E0311 -- the while can finish\n      while n > 0 \{\n          if lucky(n) \{ return 7 }\n      }\n  }\n\nFix, and this one always compiles: add a trailing `return <value>`,\nor a tail value expression.\n\n  fn first(n: Int) -> Int \{\n      while n > 0 \{ if lucky(n) \{ return 7 } }\n      0\n  }\n\nWhen \"no value\" is a real outcome, change the return type to\nOption[R] and yield None.\n\n  fn first(n: Int) -> Option[Int] \{\n      while n > 0 \{ if lucky(n) \{ return Some(7) } }\n      None\n  }\n\nA tail that provably DIVERGES is accepted and needs no repair: a\n`loop` no `break` targets, or a call whose callee is declared\n`-> Never`, such as panic(\"...\") or env.exit. The search for a targeting\n`break` does not descend into a nested loop or a closure body.\n\n  fn serve() -> Int \{             // OK -- the loop never exits\n      loop \{ if done() \{ return code() } }\n  }\n\nA function declared `-> Never` has no value to return, so its body\nmust end in such a tail.\n\n  fn fail(m: Str) -> Never \{      // OK -- panic diverges\n      panic(\"config: \{m\}\")\n  }\n\nThe tail kind (while, for, loop, arm) travels with the diagnostic as\nstructured data, so one code covers every shape."
    }
    if code == UNSOLVED_TYPEVAR_AT_CODEGEN {
        return "I0001 -- UnsolvedTypeVarAtCodegen\n\nAn INTERNAL COMPILER ERROR. A generic type's parameter was still\nunsolved when code generation tried to monomorphize it, so the\ncompiler had no concrete type to emit for that slot.\n\nThis is a bug in the compiler, not in your program. One of two\nthings should have happened first and did not:\n\n  - the type should have been inferred from a use, or\n  - the front end should have reported error[CannotInferType]\n    (E0301) for a genuinely under-determined binding.\n\nThe compiler stops here rather than emitting a type-erased name,\nwhich would silently collapse distinct instantiations onto one C\ntype and miscompile.\n\nFix: please report this, ideally with the smallest program that\nreproduces it. As a workaround, adding an explicit type annotation\nat the reported location usually avoids the unsolved slot.\n\n  let b: GKV[Int, Str] = GKV \{ m: Map() \}   // annotated -- avoids it"
    }
    if code == UNHANDLED_ITERABLE_AT_CODEGEN {
        return "I0002 -- UnhandledIterableAtCodegen\n\nAn INTERNAL COMPILER ERROR. A `for` loop's iterable passed the\nfront-end's iterable check (error[NotIterable], E0302) but code\ngeneration has no loop emitter for its runtime type.\n\nThis is a bug in the compiler, not in your program: the type is\ngenuinely one of the built-in IntoIterator kinds (List/Set/Map/\nRange/Str/Iterator) or the Channel receive extension, but the\ncorresponding codegen arm has not been implemented yet. The\ncompiler stops here rather than silently emitting a loop that runs\nzero iterations.\n\nFix: please report this, ideally with the smallest program that\nreproduces it."
    }
    if code == CODEGEN_STAGE_NOT_BUILT {
        return "I0004 -- CodegenStageNotBuilt\n\nAn INTERNAL COMPILER ERROR. The compiler reached a code-generation\nstage that this build does not contain.\n\nThe C emitters are being rewritten stage by stage. An entry point\nwhose stage has not landed yet raises this rather than returning\nempty output, so a missing stage can never be mistaken for a\nprogram that legitimately emits nothing.\n\nThis is a bug in the compiler, not in your program. Fix: use a\nreleased compiler build to compile this program."
    }
    if code == POISONED_IR_AT_SEAM {
        return "I0005 -- PoisonedIrAtSeam\n\nAn INTERNAL COMPILER ERROR. One code-generation stage handed\nanother a lowered node that does not exist.\n\nWhen a stage cannot lower something it reports the reason and\nanswers a negative id. A caller that receives one must stop and\npass it on. This fires when a caller instead handed that id to a\nproducer that has to embed it in the tree, so the real failure was\nalready reported above this one -- this second report names the\ncaller that ignored it.\n\nThe compiler stops here rather than building a tree that holds a\nnode the pool does not contain, which the C printer would read as\nif it were real.\n\nThis is a bug in the compiler, not in your program. Fix: please\nreport this, ideally with the smallest program that reproduces it,\nand include the internal error printed just before this one."
    }
    if code == NO_SOURCE_TEXT_FOR_EXPR {
        return "I0006 -- NoSourceTextForExpression\n\nAn INTERNAL COMPILER ERROR. The compiler could not rebuild the\nsource text of an expression.\n\nA failing assert reports its condition the way you wrote it. Nothing\nafter the parser keeps that text, so it is rebuilt from the parsed\nprogram. This fires when an expression kind has no rendering yet.\n\nThe compiler stops rather than inventing text, because invented text\nin a failure message would read as though you had written it.\n\nThis is a bug in the compiler, not in your program. Fix: please\nreport this, ideally with the smallest program that reproduces it."
    }
    if code == UNVERIFIED_IR_AT_PRINT {
        return "I0007 -- UnverifiedIrAtPrint\n\nAn INTERNAL COMPILER ERROR. Code generation lowered a function to\nan internal tree that the verifier refuses, and the C printer was\nabout to read it.\n\nThe printer reads that tree by position: it takes each part to be\nwhat its place says it is and writes the C for it. A tree that is\nthe wrong shape therefore becomes C that nothing later can tell\nis wrong, so the driver checks every unit before it is printed and\nstops on the first defect it finds. The message names that\ndefect.\n\nThis is a bug in the compiler, not in your program. Fix: please\nreport this, ideally with the smallest program that reproduces it."
    }
    if code == UNDECLARED_EFFECT {
        return "E0500 -- UndeclaredEffect\n\nA function calls another function that requires an effect, but the\ncaller does not declare that effect in its signature.\n\nA parent effect covers its children, a `with` block adds the effect\nof each handler it installs (`with arena` adds `Arena`), a closure\nholds the effects of the function around it, and `main` holds every\neffect.\n\nFix: add `! EffectName` to the calling function's signature, or make\nthe call inside a `with` block that provides the effect.\n\n  fn greet() ! IO \{\n      io.println(\"hello\")\n  \}\n\n  fn good() ! IO \{\n      greet()             // OK -- IO declared\n  \}\n\n  fn bad() \{\n      greet()             // E0500: greet needs IO, bad() has none\n  \}"
    }
    if code == CAPABILITY_BUDGET {
        return "E0501 -- CapabilityBudgetExceeded\n\nA function uses an effect that is not listed in its module's\n@capabilities budget. The budget caps which effects a module is\npermitted to use; calls that need an effect outside it are rejected.\n\nFix: add the effect to the @capabilities annotation at the top of\nyour module.\n\n  @capabilities(IO, Net)\n  fn fetch() ! IO, Net \{ ... \}  // OK -- both in budget"
    }
    if code == QM_INVALID_OPERAND {
        return "E0502 -- InvalidOperand for ? or ??\n\nThe `?` or `??` operator was used on a value that is not the\nexpected type. `?` requires Result or Option, `??` requires Option.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Using `?` on a plain value instead of a Result or Option\n  - Using `??` on a Result instead of an Option\n\n  let val = some_option ?? \"default\"  // OK\n  let res = try_parse()?              // OK -- ? on Result\n  let inner = some_option?            // OK -- ? on Option (in Option-returning fn)"
    }
    if code == UNDEFINED_FUNCTION {
        return "E0504 -- UndefinedFunction\n\nA function was called that has not been defined or imported.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Typo in the function name\n  - Forgetting to import the module that defines the function\n  - The function is private (not pub) in another module\n\nFix: check the function name spelling, or add the appropriate import.\n\n  import math\n  let x = math.sqrt(4.0)  // OK -- imported"
    }
    if code == UNRESOLVED_METHOD {
        return "E0505 -- UnresolvedMethod\n\nA method call could not be resolved for the receiver's type.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Calling a method that does not exist on that type\n  - The variable's type is not what you expected\n  - Missing trait implementation\n\nFix: check the receiver type and available methods.\n\n  let x: Int = 42\n  x.contains(\"a\")  // E0505: Int has no .contains() method"
    }
    if code == UNDEFINED_VARIABLE {
        return "E0506 -- UndefinedVariable\n\nA variable was referenced that has not been declared in scope.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Typo in the variable name\n  - Using a variable before it is declared\n  - Variable is out of scope (declared in a different block)\n\n  let name = \"Blink\"\n  io.println(nane)  // E0506: `nane` -- did you mean `name`?"
    }
    if code == UNKNOWN_TYPE {
        return "E0507 -- UnknownType\n\nA type name was used that the compiler does not recognize.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Typo in the type name\n  - Forgetting to define or import the type\n  - Using a type from a module that is not imported\n\n  type Point \{ x: Int, y: Int \}\n  let p: Piont = ...  // E0507: unknown type `Piont`"
    }
    if code == QM_RESULT_IN_NON_RESULT {
        return "E0508 -- QuestionMarkResultInNonResult\n\nThe `?` operator was used on a Result value inside a function that\ndoes not return Result.\n\nFix: change the function's return type to Result[T, E].\n\n  fn parse(s: Str) -> Result[Int, Str] \{ ... \}\n\n  fn caller() -> Result[Int, Str] \{\n      let n = parse(\"42\")?  // OK -- caller returns Result\n      Ok(n)\n  \}"
    }
    if code == QM_OPTION_IN_NON_OPTION {
        return "E0509 -- QuestionMarkOptionInNonOption\n\nThe `?` operator was used on an Option value inside a function that\ndoes not return Option.\n\nFix: change the function's return type to Option[T].\n\n  fn find(items: List[Str], key: Str) -> Option[Str] \{\n      items.get(0)?  // OK -- function returns Option\n  \}"
    }
    if code == QM_ERROR_MISMATCH {
        return "E0512 -- QuestionMarkErrorMismatch\n\nThe `?` operator was used on a Result whose error type does not\nmatch the enclosing function's return error type.\n\nFix: ensure both error types are the same, or convert the error.\n\n  fn parse(s: Str) -> Result[Int, ParseError] \{ ... \}\n\n  fn caller() -> Result[Str, ParseError] \{\n      let n = parse(\"42\")?  // OK -- both use ParseError\n      Ok(n.to_string())\n  \}"
    }
    if code == COALESCE_REQUIRES_OPT {
        return "E0513 -- CoalesceRequiresOption\n\nThe `??` operator requires its left operand to be an Option value.\nIt unwraps Some(v) to v, or substitutes the right-hand default for\nNone. Applying `??` to a non-Option type is rejected.\n\n  let val = maybe_name() ?? \"anonymous\"   // OK -- maybe_name returns Option[Str]\n  let bad = 42 ?? 0                         // E0513 -- 42 is Int, not Option\n\nFix: change the left operand to an Option, or remove the `??`."
    }
    if code == QM_TEST_DISPLAY_REQUIRED {
        return "E0514 -- QuestionMarkTestDisplayRequired\n\nThe `?` operator was used inside a test block on a Result whose error\ntype does not implement Display. Test bodies that use `?` are\nimplicitly elaborated to return Result[(), TestError], and each\npropagated error is rendered via Display.display(e) for the runner's\nNDJSON output. The error type must therefore implement Display.\n\nFix: add `impl Display for E` for the error type, or `.unwrap()` the\nResult explicitly inside the test.\n\n  type MyErr \{ msg: Str \}\n  impl Display for MyErr \{ fn fmt(self, sb: StringBuilder) \{ sb.write(self.msg) \} \}\n\n  test \"ok\" \{\n      let n = fallible()?  // OK once Display is implemented\n  \}"
    }
    if code == TEST_ANNOTATED_RETURN {
        return "E0515 -- AnnotatedTestReturnTypeRejected\n\nA test block declared an explicit return type with `->`. Test bodies\ndo not name their return type: if the body uses `?` anywhere, it is\nimplicitly elaborated to Result[(), TestError]; otherwise the\nimplicit return type is (). User-supplied return annotations are\nrejected to keep the rule unambiguous.\n\nFix: remove the `-> ...` annotation.\n\n  test \"x\" \{ ... \}                       // OK\n  test \"x\" -> Result[(), Str] \{ ... \}    // E0515 -- drop the arrow"
    }
    if code == NAME_COLLISION {
        return "E0518 -- NameCollision\n\nA struct type and an enum variant share the same name. Bare\nconstruction (`Name \{ ... }`) would be ambiguous: the compiler\ncould not tell whether you meant the struct or the variant, and\nsilently picking one would be a miscompile hazard.\n\nThis rule is narrow: it ONLY rejects a struct name equal to an enum\nvariant name. Two different enums may freely share a variant name\n(e.g. `Pending` in both `JobState` and `NetState`) — that case is\nlegal and resolved by the expected type at the use site.\n\nFix: rename either the struct or the variant.\n\n  type Status \{ code: Int }       // struct named `Status`\n  type Job \{ Status \{ at: Int } }  // E0518 -- variant also named `Status`"
    }
    if code == AMBIGUOUS_CONSTRUCTION {
        return "E0519 -- AmbiguousConstruction\n\nA bare variant name in value position -- nullary (`Pending`), a payload\ncall (`Running(5)`) or a brace literal (`Pending \{ id: 1 }`) -- names a\nvariant that more than one enum declares, and no expected type at the\nsite picks one. The prelude counts: a user variant spelled `Ok`, `Err`,\n`Some`, `None`, `Less`, `Equal` or `Greater` competes with Result,\nOption or Ordering. The compiler will not guess which enum you meant.\n\nFix: give the site an expected type (a binding annotation, a parameter,\na return type, the other operand of `==`), or qualify the name with the\nenum.\n\n  type JobState \{ Pending, Done }\n  type NetState \{ Pending, Closed }\n\n  let s = Pending                 // E0519 -- `Pending` is in two enums\n  let s: JobState = Pending       // OK -- annotation gives the hint\n  let s = JobState.Pending        // OK -- qualified"
    }
    if code == DERIVE_DEBUG_FIELD_NO_DEBUG {
        return "E0520 -- DeriveDebugFieldNoDebug\n\n@derive(Debug) generates a debug() that renders every field by\ncalling that field's own debug(). A field whose type is a user\nstruct or enum that does not itself derive (or implement) Debug has\nno debug() to call, so the derived method cannot be generated.\n\nFix: add @derive(Debug) to the field's type, or remove that field's\ntype from the Debug-derived type.\n\n  @derive(Debug)\n  type Inner \{ a: Int }            // OK -- Inner derives Debug\n  @derive(Debug)\n  type Outer \{ i: Inner }          // OK\n\n  type Plain \{ a: Int }            // does NOT derive Debug\n  @derive(Debug)\n  type Bad \{ p: Plain }            // E0520 -- field `p` type `Plain` has no Debug"
    }
    if code == STR_PLUS_UNSUPPORTED {
        return "E0521 -- StrPlusUnsupported\n\n`+` does not work on Str. Blink reserves `+` for numeric arithmetic;\nusing it to join strings encourages accidental O(n^2) build-up loops\nand creates ambiguity with numeric `+`. Str deliberately does not\nimplement Add.\n\nFix: use string interpolation to embed a value, or .concat() to join\ntwo strings.\n\n  let name = \"world\"\n  let greeting = \"hello \{name\}\"     // OK -- interpolation\n  let joined = a.concat(b)            // OK -- .concat()\n\n  let bad = a + b                     // E0521 -- `+` on Str\n  let mixed = \"x\" + 1                 // E0521 -- `+` on Str"
    }
    if code == AMBIGUOUS_METHOD_CALL {
        return "E0522 -- AmbiguousMethodCall\n\nAn unqualified method call `x.m()` resolved to a method `m` defined\nby two or more traits that the receiver's type implements. The\ncompiler cannot tell which trait's method you meant.\n\nFix: use a qualified call `Trait.m(receiver)` to name the trait.\n\n  trait Serializable \{ fn serialize(self) -> Str }\n  trait Debuggable   \{ fn serialize(self) -> Str }\n  impl Serializable for Config \{ fn serialize(self) -> Str \{ ... } }\n  impl Debuggable   for Config \{ fn serialize(self) -> Str \{ ... } }\n\n  c.serialize()                // E0522 -- found in two traits\n  Serializable.serialize(c)    // OK -- qualified\n  Debuggable.serialize(c)      // OK -- qualified\n\nAn ASSOCIATED function -- one declared without a receiver, such as\n`fn make() -> Self` -- is reported the same way and has no qualified\nform to fall back on: `Trait.make()` names the trait by passing the\nreceiver first, and this call has no receiver to pass.\n\nFix: rename one of the methods, or drop one of the impls.\n\n  trait Alpha \{ fn make() -> Self }\n  trait Beta  \{ fn make() -> Self }\n  impl Alpha for Widget \{ fn make() -> Self \{ ... } }\n  impl Beta  for Widget \{ fn make() -> Self \{ ... } }\n\n  Widget.make()                // E0522 -- declared by two traits"
    }
    if code == MISSING_DISPLAY_IMPL {
        return "E0523 -- MissingDisplayImpl\n\nAn interpolated expression `\{expr}` in a string literal requires its\ntype to implement Display. Interpolation renders the value via\nDisplay.display(); there is no fallback to Debug and no auto-\nsynthesis of a representation.\n\nBuiltins with a compiler-provided Display: Int, Float, Bool, Str,\nChar (and tuples of them). Collections such as List, Map and Option\nare Debug-only, not Display, so interpolating them is also an error.\n\nFix: add `@derive(Display)` or implement Display manually, or use\n`.debug()` for a structural representation.\n\n  @derive(Display)\n  type Point \{ x: Int, y: Int }\n  io.println(\"p = \{p}\")          // OK once Display exists\n\n  type Bare \{ x: Int }\n  io.println(\"p = \{b}\")          // E0523 -- no Display impl\n  io.println(\"p = \{b.debug()}\")  // OK -- structural via Debug"
    }
    if code == RESERVED_TYPE_NAME {
        return "E0524 -- ReservedTypeName\n\nA `type`, type alias, `trait` or `effect` declaration took a reserved\nname. The reserved names are `Self`, the scalar types that literal\nsyntax produces (`Int`, `I8`-`I64`, `U8`-`U64`, `Float`, `F32`, `F64`,\n`Str`, `Char`, `Bool`) and `Void` (sections/07 §10.6). Literals\nproduce these types in every module, so a module that declared its own\n`Int` could not write an integer literal of either type. The rule holds\nin every module, including one that another module imports.\n\nFor now `Fn` and `Tuple` are reserved too: the compiler writes them as\nthe head of a function or tuple type.\n\nThe declaration is dropped, so the builtin keeps the name.\n\nFix: choose another name.\n\n  type Int \{ v: Int }        // E0524 -- reserved name\n  type IntValue \{ v: Int }   // OK\n\nEvery other compiler-known type name, such as `Option` or `Handler`,\nmay be declared; that is warning W1010."
    }
    if code == NO_SUCH_VARIANT {
        return "E0547 -- NoSuchVariant\n\nA variant pattern, or a qualified variant construction, named a variant\nthat its enum does not declare (sections/03_types.md §3.5 Pattern Forms,\n§3.4 Variant Construction). A qualified `Q.V` where `Q` is a type that\nis not an enum is E0547 as well, in a pattern and in an expression,\nwhatever the type being matched: `Q` declares no variant. The header\nsays what `Q` is.\n\n  match n \{\n      Int.Zed => 1         // E0547 -- `Int` is not an enum\n      _ => 0\n  \}\n  let p = Point.Zed(1)     // E0547 -- `Point` is a struct\n\nA bare uppercase name, or a bare name with a payload, at a position\nwhose type is not an enum is E0308, not E0547.\n\n  type Shape \{ Circle(Float), Dot \}\n  match s \{\n      Circel(r) => r     // E0547 -- did you mean `Circle`?\n      Shape.Dott => 0.0  // E0547 -- did you mean `Dot`?\n  \}\n  let k = Shape.Squar(1.0)  // E0547 -- the value form is checked too\n\nThe check runs at every depth: in `Some(Somee(v))` the inner pattern is\nchecked against the payload type of `Some`. The span is the variant\nname. When one declared variant is near the name, the help names it, and\nthe rename is a machine fix when the payload forms agree (no payload and\na unit variant, `( )` and `( )`, `\{ \}` and `\{ \}`). Otherwise the\nhelp lists the variants. When exactly one other visible enum declares a\nbare name, a note names it; the pattern still names a variant of the\ntype being matched.\n\nThe order of checks is: resolve a qualifier (E0507), look the variant up\nin it (E0547), then compare with the type being matched (E0300). So\n`Other.Q(v)` on a `T` value, where `Other` declares `Q`, is E0300.\n\nAn arm with a bad pattern covers nothing. When it has no guard, the match\ngets no E0004, and its bindings have the error type, so their uses report\nnothing more.\n\nFix: name a variant the enum declares. Adding a variant to the enum is\nalmost never the right fix for a misspelled name."
    }
    if code == NO_SUCH_FIELD {
        return "E0525 -- NoSuchField\n\nA field access or a literal's label named a field the type does not\ndeclare.\n\nMember access is checked against the declared shape: sections/03_types.md\nmakes `pair.2` on a two-element tuple a compile error, and the same rule\napplies to a struct field the declaration never lists. It also covers an\n@ffi.opaque handle, which has no body and therefore no fields (§9.1.4). A\ntype parameter declares no fields either, and a bound adds only methods,\nso `x.f` on a value of type T is rejected in T's own body (§3\nParametricity). Every other value -- a Str, an Int, a List, an enum --\ndeclares no fields, so `s.0` or `n.name` on it is E0525 as well.\n\n  type P \{ a: Int \}\n  let p = P \{ a: 1 \}\n  p.zzz   // E0525 -- P has no field `zzz`\n  p.a     // OK\n\n  fn f[T](x: T) -> Int \{ x.a \}          // E0525 -- T has no field `a`\n  fn g[T: HasA](x: T) -> Int \{ x.a() \}  // OK -- a method of the bound\n\nA label in a struct literal, a variant brace literal or a labelled\nvariant paren payload is checked the same way (sections/02_syntax.md\n§2.14). When one declared field the literal does not yet label is\nnearest the label, ignoring case and `_`, the help names it and the\nrename is a machine fix; that field is then not also reported missing.\nOtherwise the help lists the declared fields and marks those given.\n\n  type Point \{ x: Float, y: Float \}\n  let p = Point \{ x: 1.0, yy: 2.0 \}   // E0525 -- did you mean `y`?\n\nA label in a struct pattern or a variant brace pattern is checked the\nsame way (sections/03_types.md §3.5). For a punned label the fix writes\nthe field and keeps the binder, so `Point \{ x, yy \}` becomes\n`Point \{ x, y: yy \}`.\n\nFix: use a declared field, or add the field to the type when the type is\ndeclared in the package being compiled. A pattern cannot add a field."
    }
    if code == DUPLICATE_EFFECT_OP {
        return "E0516 -- DuplicateEffectOperation\n\nTwo sub-effects under one root effect declare an operation of the same\nname. The operations of one root effect share a single dispatch table,\nso every operation name under that root must be unique.\n\nFix: rename one of the operations, or move it under a different root\neffect.\n\n  effect Db \{\n      effect Read \{ fn query(sql: Str) -> Str \}\n      effect Write \{ fn exec(sql: Str) \}   // OK -- distinct names\n  \}\n\n  effect Bad \{\n      effect A \{ fn run() \}\n      effect B \{ fn run() \}                 // E0516 -- `run` repeated\n  \}"
    }
    if code == OVERLAPPING_IMPLS {
        return "E0526 -- OverlappingImpls\n\nTwo impls of one trait could both match the same type. Blink has no\nspecialization: there is no \"more specific impl wins\" rule, so a call\non a type both impls answer for has no impl to pick\n(sections/03_types.md \"No Impl Overlap\").\n\nTwo shapes overlap:\n  - The same trait implemented twice for the same type.\n  - A blanket impl and a concrete one over the same container, when\n    the concrete type argument satisfies the blanket impl's bounds.\n    `impl[T] R for List[T] where T: Display` and `impl R for\n    List[Int]` overlap, because Int implements Display.\n\nFix: keep one impl, or wrap one of the types in a newtype so the two\nimpls answer for types that share no instance.\n\n  impl[T] R for List[T] where T: Display \{ ... }\n  impl R for List[Int] \{ ... }        // E0526 -- List[Int] matches both\n\n  type IntList \{ xs: List[Int] }\n  impl R for IntList \{ ... }          // OK -- a type of its own"
    }
    if code == ARENA_VALUE_ESCAPES {
        return "E0700 -- ArenaValueEscapes\n\nA value allocated inside a `with arena \{ }` block escapes the\narena's scope. Arena-allocated memory is freed when the arena exits,\nso references that survive the block would dangle.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Storing an arena value into a long-lived field or global\n  - Capturing an arena value in a closure that outlives the block\n  - Passing an arena value to a function that keeps it\n\nThe diagnostic prints the resolved promotion target — either\n`promoted into: outer arena` (when the escaping site is inside a\nnested `with arena`) or `promoted into: GC heap` (outermost block) —\nso nested-arena escapes are debuggable without guessing the shape of\nthe enclosing scope.\n\nFix: remove `! Arena` and let the value be GC-managed, or copy the\nvalue explicitly before it leaves the arena scope."
    }
    if code == ARENA_CLOSURE_TAIL_UNSUPPORTED {
        return "E0702 -- ArenaClosureTailUnsupported\n\nThe tail of `with arena \{ ... \}` is a closure, so the closure and\nwhat it captures are copied out of the arena before the arena is\nfreed. Each capture is copied through the walker for its type,\nincluding a handler and a Template. A closure tail is refused when\nthat copy cannot be made:\n\n  E0702a  the closure is not a literal made in the block\n\nA capture whose type holds itself reports E0701 instead. E0702d is\nkept for a capture type with no walker; no type reports it today.\n\nFix: build the closure outside the `with arena \{ \}` block, or\nreturn a value that is not a closure and build the closure after."
    }
    if code == PRIVATE_ITEM_ACCESS {
        return "E1003 -- PrivateItemAccess\n\nAn attempt was made to access a private (non-pub) item from another\nmodule.\n\nFix: either mark the item as `pub` in its module, or use the\nmodule's public API instead.\n\n  // in math.bl\n  pub fn sqrt(x: Float) -> Float \{ ... \}  // accessible\n  fn helper() \{ ... \}                      // private"
    }
    if code == VERSION_CONFLICT {
        return "E1004 -- VersionConflict\n\nTwo dependencies in the lockfile require different versions of the\nsame package. Blink enforces exactly one version per package.\n\n  myapp depends on http 0.5\n  auth 1.0 depends on http 0.6\n  → conflict: only one version of `http` allowed\n\nFix: run `blink update` to find a compatible version, or pin\ndependencies to use the same version in blink.toml."
    }
    if code == INVALID_MODULE_ANNOTATION {
        return "E1008 -- InvalidModuleAnnotation\n\nThe @module annotation tried to move a file into a different package.\n@module can only set the module name to the parent package name —\nyou cannot use it to move a file into an unrelated package.\n\n  // auth/login.bl\n  @module(\"auth\")     // OK — matches parent package `auth`\n  @module(\"billing\")  // E1008 — file is in `auth`, not `billing`\n\nFix: use @module with the parent package name, or remove the annotation."
    }
    if code == PACKAGE_ENTRY_NOT_FOUND {
        return "E1009 -- PackageEntryNotFound\n\nA bare `import <pkg>` resolved to a package whose entry file is\nmissing. The entry file is mandatory and must live at\n<pkg-root>/src/<name>.bl, where <name> is [package].name.\n\n  // libs/pg/blink.toml\n  // [package]\n  // name = \"pg\"\n  // libs/pg/src/pg.bl     <-- expected\n\nFix: create src/<name>.bl in the package, or correct the\n[package].name field in blink.toml."
    }
    if code == ORPHAN_FILE {
        return "E1010 -- OrphanFile\n\nA source file with no enclosing blink.toml is not part of any\npackage. Bare external imports from such a file are not allowed —\nonly stdlib (std.*) and bundled (pkg.*) imports work.\n\n  // /tmp/foo.bl  (no blink.toml ancestor)\n  import mylib    // E1010 — orphan file\n\nFix: create a blink.toml in an ancestor directory to declare the\npackage, or import only std.* / pkg.* modules."
    }
    if code == INVALID_PACKAGE_NAME {
        return "E1011 -- InvalidPackageName\n\nThe [package].name field violates the package-name grammar.\n[package].name must match [a-z][a-z0-9_]* — start with a lowercase\nletter, followed by lowercase letters, digits, or underscores.\nHyphens, dots, and uppercase letters are forbidden.\n\n  name = \"my_lib\"   // OK\n  name = \"My-Lib\"   // E1011 — uppercase + hyphen\n  name = \"1lib\"     // E1011 — leading digit\n\nFix: rename the package to satisfy the grammar."
    }
    if code == PACKAGE_NOT_DECLARED {
        return "E1052 -- PackageNotDeclared\n\nA Tier 2 stdlib package was imported without declaring it in blink.toml.\nTier 2 packages (std.http, std.db, std.log, std.config, std.net) require\nexplicit dependency declaration.\n\n  // blink.toml\n  [dependencies]\n  std/http = \"0.1\"\n\n  // then in your code:\n  import std.http  // OK with declaration\n\nTier 1 packages (std.json, std.toml, etc.) are always available."
    }
    if code == DUPLICATE_MODULE_BINDING {
        return "E1016 -- DuplicateModuleBinding\n\nA module declares one name twice in one namespace. Module scope has\ntwo namespaces: values (let, let mut, const, fn) and types (type, type\nalias, trait, effect). Module scope is flat, so neither declaration is\ninner to the other and a bare name cannot say which one it means. The\nkinds and signatures do not matter: two fns of one name collide even\nwhen their parameters differ, because Blink has no overloading. A type\nand a fn of one name do not collide.\n\n  let x = 1\n  let x = 2                        // E1016 -- `x` is declared twice\n  fn f() -> Int \{ 1 \}\n  fn f(n: Int) -> Int \{ n \}       // E1016 -- `f` is declared twice\n  type Shape \{ r: Int \}\n  trait Shape \{ fn area(self) -> Int \}   // E1016\n\nFix: rename one of the two declarations. The compiler cannot know\nwhich one to rename, so it applies no fix.\n\nA module-scope let, let mut, const or fn must also not take the name\nof a variant of an enum in the same package, or of a prelude enum\n(Option, Result, Ordering). A bare name has one meaning at each site.\nA variant of a dependency's enum does not collide: there the value\ncomes first at a bare use.\n\n  type Color \{ Red, Green \}\n  fn Red() -> Int \{ 1 \}           // E1016 -- collides with Color.Red\n  let Ok = 5                       // E1016 -- collides with Result.Ok\n\nFix: rename the value. See section 2.12.1."
    }
    if code == INLINE_MODULE_NOT_SUPPORTED {
        return "E1015 -- InlineModuleNotSupported\n\nInline modules (mod name \{ ... \}) are not supported in Blink.\nBlink uses file-based modules: one file = one module.\n\nFix: move the module contents into a separate file and use import.\n\n  // Instead of:\n  mod schema \{\n      fn validate() \{ ... \}\n  \}\n\n  // Create schema.bl with the contents and import it:\n  import schema\n  schema.validate()\n\nSee section 10.1 for details on Blink's module system."
    }
    if code == UNEXPECTED_TOKEN {
        return "E1100 -- UnexpectedToken\n\nThe parser encountered a token it did not expect at this position.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Missing or extra braces, parentheses, or brackets\n  - Incorrect syntax for the current context\n  - Using an operator in the wrong position\n\nFix: check the line and column indicated and look for syntax errors."
    }
    if code == UNEXPECTED_TOKEN_STRING {
        return "E1101 -- UnexpectedTokenString\n\nThe parser encountered an unexpected token inside a string\ninterpolation.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Malformed interpolation expression\n  - Unmatched braces inside a string\n\nFix: ensure interpolation expressions are valid Blink expressions."
    }
    if code == UNEXPECTED_TOKEN_PATTERN {
        return "E1102 -- UnexpectedTokenPattern\n\nThe parser encountered an unexpected token inside a match pattern.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Invalid pattern syntax in a match arm\n  - Using expressions where a pattern is expected\n\nFix: use valid pattern syntax: literals, identifiers, enum variants,\nor wildcard `_`."
    }
    if code == KEYWORD_AS_IDENTIFIER {
        return "E1103 -- KeywordAsIdentifier\n\nA language keyword was used where an identifier (variable or\nfunction name) was expected.\n\nKeywords: fn, let, if, else, match, while, return, type, trait,\nimport, pub, handler, effect, enum, for, in, mut, const, test\n\nFix: choose a different name that is not a reserved keyword.\n\n  let result = 42    // OK\n  let match = 42     // E1103: `match` is a keyword"
    }
    if code == INTERP_PARSE_FAILURE {
        return "E1106 -- InterpParseFailure\n\nAn interpolation expression inside a \"...\" string failed to\nparse. Every unescaped `\{` in a \"...\" string starts an\ninterpolation expression — even between backticks, which have no\nspecial meaning in strings.\n\nFix: for a literal brace, escape it as `\\\{`. For prose-heavy\ntext, use an extended string #\"...\"# where braces are literal\nand interpolation uses #\{expr\}.\n\n  \"usage: `f() \\\{ ... \}`\"      // \\\{ escapes the brace\n  #\"usage: `f() \{ ... \}`\"#     // braces are literal"
    }
    if code == EMPTY_BRACE_EXPR {
        return "E1107 -- EmptyBraceExpr\n\nEmpty braces were used in expression position. Blink does not have\nempty-brace map syntax.\n\nFix: use Map() to construct an empty map.\n\n  let m: Map[Str, Int] = Map()  // OK"
    }
    if code == FILE_NOT_FOUND {
        return "E1108 -- FileNotFound\n\nAn #embed() compile-time intrinsic referenced a file that does not\nexist relative to the source file.\n\nFix: check that the file path is correct relative to the source\nfile.\n\n  const data: Str = #embed(\"data.txt\")  // file must exist"
    }
    if code == MUT_FIELD_NOT_SUPPORTED {
        return "E1109 -- MutFieldNotSupported\n\nThe `mut` keyword was used on a struct field declaration. In Blink,\nmutability is a property of the binding, not the type or its fields.\nAll fields of a `let mut` binding are mutable; all fields of a `let`\nbinding are immutable.\n\nFix: remove `mut` from the field and use `let mut` when binding.\n\n  type Point \{ x: Float, y: Float \}\n  let mut p = Point \{ x: 1.0, y: 2.0 \}\n  p.x = 3.0  // OK — binding is mut"
    }
    if code == UNEXPECTED_ANNOTATION {
        return "E1110 -- UnexpectedAnnotation\n\nAn annotation was used in a position where it is not supported. For\nexample, only @where is permitted on type aliases.\n\nFix: remove the annotation or move it to a construct that supports\nit. For type aliases, use @where(predicate) for refinement\nconstraints."
    }
    if code == UNKNOWN_INTRINSIC {
        return "E1111 -- UnknownIntrinsic\n\nThe parser encountered a compile-time intrinsic (#name) that is not\nrecognized. The only currently-supported intrinsic is #embed.\n\nFix: use a supported intrinsic, or remove the call.\n\n  const data: Str = #embed(\"data.txt\")  // OK"
    }
    if code == UNEXPECTED_CHARACTER {
        return "E1113 -- UnexpectedCharacter\n\nThe lexer encountered a character it does not recognize as part of\nany Blink token. The most common cause is a stray `;` left over\nfrom another language — Blink uses newlines as statement terminators\nand spec section 2.5 explicitly forbids semicolons.\n\nFix: remove the offending character. If it is `;`, just delete it;\nthe newline that follows already terminates the statement.\n\n  let x = 3;        // E1113 -- delete the `;`\n  let x = 3         // OK"
    }
    if code == EMPTY_UNICODE_ESCAPE {
        return "E1114 -- EmptyUnicodeEscape\n\nA `\\u\{H\}` escape names a Unicode scalar value by its hex code, and\nthis one has no hex digits between the braces.\n\n  let s = \"a\\u\{\}b\"      // E1114\n  let s = \"a\\u\{41\}b\"    // OK -- \"aAb\"\n\nFix: write 1 to 6 hex digits between the braces."
    }
    if code == UNICODE_ESCAPE_TOO_LONG {
        return "E1115 -- UnicodeEscapeTooLong\n\nA `\\u\{H\}` escape takes 1 to 6 hex digits, leading zeros included.\nThe largest Unicode scalar value, 0x10FFFF, needs six.\n\n  let s = \"\\u\{0001F600\}\"   // E1115 -- 8 digits\n  let s = \"\\u\{1F600\}\"      // OK\n\nFix: drop the leading zeros, or write at most 6 hex digits."
    }
    if code == INVALID_UNICODE_ESCAPE_DIGIT {
        return "E1116 -- InvalidUnicodeEscapeDigit\n\nThe text between the braces of a `\\u\{H\}` escape must be hex digits:\n`0-9`, `a-f` or `A-F`. The message names the first character that is\nnot one.\n\n  let s = \"\\u\{12g\}\"    // E1116 -- `g` is not a hex digit\n  let s = \"\\u\{12f\}\"    // OK\n\nFix: write the code point in hex."
    }
    if code == UNTERMINATED_UNICODE_ESCAPE {
        return "E1117 -- UnterminatedUnicodeEscape\n\nA `\\u\{` escape runs until its closing `\}`, and this one has none\nbefore the hex digits end.\n\n  let s = \"\\u\{41 b\"     // E1117\n  let s = \"\\u\{41\} b\"    // OK\n\nFix: close the escape with `\}`."
    }
    if code == SURROGATE_ESCAPE {
        return "E1118 -- SurrogateEscape\n\nA `\\u\{H\}` escape must name a Unicode scalar value. The surrogate code\npoints 0xD800-0xDFFF are not scalar values: UTF-16 uses them in\npairs to encode one character above 0xFFFF, and they cannot appear\non their own in a `Char` or a `Str`.\n\n  let c = '\\u\{d83d\}'      // E1118\n  let c = '\\u\{1f600\}'     // OK -- write the character, not its UTF-16 halves\n\nFix: write the code point of the whole character."
    }
    if code == UNICODE_ESCAPE_OUT_OF_RANGE {
        return "E1119 -- UnicodeEscapeOutOfRange\n\nUnicode ends at 0x10FFFF, so a `\\u\{H\}` escape cannot name a value\nabove it.\n\n  let s = \"\\u\{110000\}\"   // E1119\n  let s = \"\\u\{10ffff\}\"   // OK -- the largest scalar value\n\nFix: check the code point you meant."
    }
    if code == UNICODE_ESCAPE_NEEDS_BRACES {
        return "E1120 -- UnicodeEscapeNeedsBraces\n\nBlink writes a Unicode escape only as `\\u\{H\}`. The forms other\nlanguages use without braces, `\\uXXXX` and `\\UXXXXXXXX`, are errors,\nso their digits can never run into the text that follows.\n\n  let s = \"\\u00e9\"        // E1120\n  let s = \"\\U0001F600\"    // E1120\n  let s = \"\\u\{e9\}\"       // OK\n  let s = \"\\u\{1f600\}\"    // OK\n\nFix: put the hex digits in braces. The help shows the escape to write."
    }
    if code == HEX_ESCAPE_NOT_SUPPORTED {
        return "E1121 -- HexEscapeNotSupported\n\nBlink has no `\\x` escape. In other languages `\\xNN` means a byte in\nsome places and a code point in others; a Blink `Str` holds Unicode\ntext, so its one numeric escape names a code point.\n\n  let esc = \"\\x1b[0m\"      // E1121\n  let esc = \"\\u\{1b\}[0m\"    // OK\n\nFix: write `\\u\{NN\}` with the same hex digits. For raw bytes, use\n`Bytes`."
    }
    if code == NUL_IN_STR {
        return "E1122 -- NulInStr\n\nA `Str` cannot hold NUL (U+0000), so neither `\\0` nor `\\u\{0\}` is\nvalid in a string literal. A char literal can hold it: `'\\0'` and\n`'\\u\{0\}'` are the same `Char`.\n\n  let s = \"a\\0b\"     // E1122\n\nFix: use `Bytes` for data that contains NUL."
    }
    if code == UNKNOWN_ESCAPE {
        return "E1123 -- UnknownEscape\n\nThe escape is not valid in this literal kind. The help lists the\nvalid escapes:\n\n  string:  \\n \\r \\t \\\\ \\b \\f \\u\{H\} \\\" \\\{ \\\}\n  char:    \\n \\r \\t \\\\ \\b \\f \\0 \\u\{H\} \\'\n\nWhere the escape means something in another language, the help gives\nthe Blink fix first:\n\n  \\e \\a \\v              write `\\u\{1b\}`, `\\u\{7\}`, `\\u\{b\}`\n  \"\\'\" (in a string)    write a plain `'`\n  '\\\"' (in a char)      write a plain `\"`: `'\"'`\n  \\d \\w \\s and the other regex classes\n                        use a raw string, where `\\` is literal: `#\"\\d\"#`\n\nThe compile still fails. The lexer drops the whole escape and goes on,\nso no other error follows from it.\n\n  let s = \"\\d+\"        // E1123\n  let s = #\"\\d+\"#      // OK"
    }
    if code == EMPTY_CHAR_LITERAL {
        return "E1124 -- EmptyCharLiteral\n\nA char literal holds exactly one character, and `''` holds none. Most\noften the character meant is a space or a quote.\n\n  let c = ''      // E1124\n  let c = ' '     // OK -- a space\n  let c = '\\''    // OK -- a single quote\n  let s = \"\"      // OK -- the empty `Str`\n\nFix: write one character between the quotes, or use `\"\"` for an empty\n`Str`."
    }
    if code == MULTI_CHAR_LITERAL {
        return "E1125 -- MultiCharLiteral\n\nA char literal holds exactly one Unicode scalar value, and this one\nholds more. A char literal cannot span lines: with no closing `'` on\nthe line, its text runs to the end of the line.\n\n  let s = 'ab'      // E1125\n  let s = \"ab\"      // OK -- a `Str`\n\nFix: use double quotes for a `Str`. The help shows the literal in\nthat form."
    }
    if code == MIXED_OPERATOR_PRECEDENCE {
        return "E1127 -- MixedOperatorPrecedence\n\nArithmetic (+ - * / %), shifts (<< >>), `&`, `^` and `|` are five\noperator groups with no order among them. An operand of an operator in\none group cannot be a bare operation of another group, because\nlanguages disagree on which applies first and every grouping\ntype-checks. The help shows the grouping C uses first, then the other.\n\n  let e = x << n + 1           // E1127\n  let e = x << (n + 1)         // OK\n  let f = v << 24 | v << 16    // E1127\n  let f = (v << 24) | (v << 16) // OK\n\nOne group chains without parentheses (`a | b | c`, `x << 1 >> 2`,\n`a + b * c`), and any group may be a bare operand of a comparison:\n`flags & 15 == 0` is `(flags & 15) == 0`.\n\nFix: add the parentheses the help shows for the grouping you meant."
    }
    if code == INTERP_DOUBLE_BRACE {
        return "E1130 -- InterpDoubleBrace\n\nThe first token of a string hole's expression is `\{`. Whitespace\nbefore it does not matter. The rule holds in every string form\n(\"...\", t\"...\", #\"...\"#, t#\"...\"#) and at every depth, so\n`\{\{x\}\}` reads as neither a literal brace nor a hole that holds the\nblock `\{x\}`.\n\n  \"a \{\{x\}\} b\"            // E1130\n  \"a \\\{\{x\}\} b\"           // OK -- `\{` then the hole \{x\}: \"a \{5\} b\"\n  #\"a \{\{x\}\} b\"#          // OK -- no hole: the text \"a \{\{x\}\} b\"\n  \"\{(\{ x \})\}\"            // OK -- a block in parentheses\n\nFix: for a literal brace, write `\\\{` or use #\"...\"#. To start the\nexpression with a block, wrap the block in ( )."
    }
    if code == UNTERMINATED_LITERAL {
        return "E1126 -- UnterminatedLiteral\n\nA literal has no closer. A string, template or raw string runs to the\nend of the file; a char literal runs to the end of its line. The\nerror points at the opening delimiter, a note says where the literal\nran out, and the help names the exact closer to write: `'`, `\"`, or\n`\"` followed by as many `#` as the opening delimiter has.\n\n  let s = \"abc         // E1126 -- close the literal with `\"`\n  let r = ##\"a\"#b     // E1126 -- close the literal with `\"##`\n  let c = 'a          // E1126 -- close the literal with `'`\n\nNothing past the opening delimiter of an unclosed string is checked,\nbecause all of it was read as the string's text.\n\nFix: add the closer the help names."
    }
    if code == MODULE_NOT_FOUND {
        return "E1200 -- ModuleNotFound\n\nAn import statement referenced a module that could not be found.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Typo in the module name\n  - Missing file: the module's .bl file does not exist\n  - Incorrect path in the module hierarchy\n\nFix: verify the module name matches a .bl file in your project or\nthe standard library.\n\n  import math       // looks for lib/std/math.bl or src/math.bl\n  import pkg.audit  // looks for lib/pkg/audit.bl"
    }
    if code == INVALID_STRING_BACKED_ENUM {
        return "E1201 -- InvalidStringBackedEnum\n\nA string-backed enum variant was assigned a value that is not a\nplain string literal. Only simple string literals are allowed for\nthe storage value of a string-backed enum.\n\nFix: use a plain string literal.\n\n  enum Status \{\n      Active = \"active\"        // OK\n      Frozen = \"frozen\"        // OK\n      // Bad = some_const      // E1201 -- not a literal\n  \}"
    }
    if code == UNKNOWN_METHOD {
        return "W0501 -- UnknownMethod\n\nA method name resolves to nothing anywhere in the program, and the\nreceiver's type is not known at the call. No type can be named as\nlacking the method, so this is a warning and not an error.\n\nWhen the receiver type IS known, the same call is a hard error:\nE0505 UnresolvedMethod.\n\n  fn f[T](x: T) \{\n      x.frobnicate()   // W0501 -- T is a bare type parameter here\n  \}\n\n  let xs = [1, 2, 3]\n  xs.frobnicate()      // E0505 -- the receiver is List[Int]\n\nCommon causes:\n  - A typo in the method name\n  - The receiver has no annotation and nothing pins its type\n\nFix: annotate the receiver so the method can be resolved, or correct\nthe method name."
    }
    if code == INCOMPLETE_STATE_RESTORE {
        return "W0550 -- IncompleteStateRestore\n\nA call sits between a speculative save/restore pair that restores\nsome of the globals the callee writes, and the callee also writes\nglobals that no pair around the call restores.\n\nThe lint reads the callee's return write set: the globals it writes\non some path that reaches its normal return. A write on a path that\nends in a call to a `-> Never` function (`panic`, `env.exit`, or your\nown) does not count, because the caller never runs on after it.\n\nA save is `let x = G` or `let x = G.clone()` for a module `let mut G`;\na restore is a later `G = x`. The pair is speculative at the call\nwhen nothing assigns G between the save and the call:\n\n  let saved = pos\n  let t = item()     // W0550 if `item` also writes `err`\n  pos = saved\n\nA pair is a binding when G gets a new value after the save and before\nthe call. The callee's writes are then the intended effect, and the\nlint does not check the call against that pair:\n\n  let saved = hint\n  hint = -1\n  let t = infer()    // no warning\n  hint = saved\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Adding a new global to a function's write-set without updating\n    the save/restore pattern in the caller\n\nFix: save and restore the globals the warning names around the call,\nor end the path that writes them in a call to a `-> Never` function.\nIf the function sets a global for this call, assign that global\nbetween its save and the call."
    }
    if code == UNRESTORED_MUTATION {
        return "W0551 -- UnrestoredMutation\n\nA call writes globals that no speculative save/restore pair around it\nrestores, and the function holds at least one speculative pair. The\nwarning's note names that pair, and a note names where the callee\nwrites the first such global, with the calls that lead there.\n\nThe lint reads the callee's return write set: the globals it writes\non some path that reaches its normal return. A write on a path that\nends in a call to a `-> Never` function (`panic`, `env.exit`, or your\nown) does not count:\n\n  fn die(msg: Str) -> Never \{\n      err = msg\n      env.exit(1)\n  \}\n\nA save is `let x = G` or `let x = G.clone()` for a module `let mut G`;\na restore is a later `G = x`. A binding pair (save, a new value in G,\na call, restore) does not count: the lint does not check a call in\na binding against that pair:\n\n  let saved = depth\n  let t = infer()    // W0551 if `infer` writes `count`\n  depth = saved\n\nA snapshot with no restore (`let before = count`) is not a pair.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Calling a function with broad side effects speculatively\n  - Forgetting to wrap the call in a save/restore block\n\nFix: save and restore the globals the warning names around the call,\nor end the path that writes them in a call to a `-> Never` function.\nIf the function sets a global for this call, assign that global\nbetween its save and the call."
    }
    if code == UNUSED_VARIABLE {
        return "W0600 -- UnusedVariable\n\nA variable was declared but never read.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Leftover variable from refactoring\n  - Variable assigned but result never used\n\nFix: remove the variable, or prefix its name with `_` to suppress\nthis warning.\n\n  let _unused = compute()  // OK -- prefixed with `_`"
    }
    if code == SET_BUT_NOT_READ {
        return "W0601 -- SetButNotRead\n\nA variable was assigned a value that was never read before being\noverwritten or going out of scope.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Redundant assignment before reassignment\n  - Leftover intermediate computation\n\nFix: remove the unused assignment, or prefix with `_` to suppress.\n\n  let mut x = compute()  // W0601: value never read\n  x = other()\n  io.println(x)"
    }
    if code == PARTIAL_HANDLER {
        return "W0545 -- PartialHandler\n\nA handler expression for an effect marked `@handle_all`, or for one of\nits sub-effects, omits a primitive operation of the effect it handles.\nAll operations of such an effect read one resource, such as one random\nstream or one clock. An omitted operation goes to the enclosing handler,\nso the handler mixes two streams.\n\nA derived operation (one with a body in the effect declaration) never\ncounts. A handler for a sub-effect counts only that sub-effect's\noperations. A handler for a marked effect counts every operation of\nevery sub-effect. A handler for an unmarked parent effect counts a\nmarked sub-effect only when it implements one or more of its\noperations. The standard library marks the builtin `Time` effect and the\n`IO.Read` sub-effect.\n\n  @handle_all\n  effect Dice \{\n      fn roll(sides: Int) -> Int\n      fn coin() -> Bool\n  \}\n\n  handler Dice \{\n      fn roll(sides: Int) -> Int \{ 6 \}   // W0545 -- omits `coin`\n  \}\n\nFix: write the operation. Any body counts; `default.coin()` forwards on\npurpose. `blink fix` adds each omitted operation as such a forward.\n`@allow(PartialHandler)` and the [lints] table also silence it."
    }
    if code == UNUSED_IMPORT {
        return "W0602 -- UnusedImport\n\nA module was imported but no symbols from it are referenced.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Leftover import from refactoring\n  - Import added for future use\n\nFix: remove the unused import statement.\n\n  import math    // W0602 if math.* never used\n  import strings // OK if strings.contains() is called"
    }
    if code == SHADOWED_DEPENDENCY {
        return "W1000 -- ShadowedDependency\n\nAn import matches both a module in the local src/ and a dependency.\nThe local module wins (sections/07 §10.5). This lets a package wrap a\ndependency without a change to the imports that use it, but a reader\nwho expects the library gets the local file.\n\n  // src/http.bl exists, and blink.toml declares a dependency `http`\n  import http                  // W1000 -- resolves to src/http.bl\n\nFix: if the shadow is not intended, rename the local module. To keep\nthe shadow without the warning, set `W1000 = \"off\"` in [lints]."
    }
    if code == UNKNOWN_MANIFEST_SECTION {
        return "W1001 -- UnknownManifestSection\n\nblink.toml has a top-level section that the spec does not define\n(sections/06 §8.9.8). blink ignores it, so a misspelled section loses\nwhat is in it: the dependencies under `[dependecies]` are not loaded,\nand the import of one fails with ModuleNotFound (E1200), which then\nnotes the unknown section.\n\n  [dependecies]                # W1001 -- did you mean `[dependencies]`?\n  local/utils = \{ path = \"../utils\" \}\n\nIt is a warning, so a manifest written for a newer toolchain still\nbuilds. --strict and a [lints] entry can make it an error.\n\nFix: correct the section name. To keep a section that this toolchain\ndoes not know without the warning, set `W1001 = \"off\"` in [lints]."
    }
    if code == SHADOWED_PRELUDE_NAME {
        return "W1010 -- ShadowedPreludeName\n\nA module declares a trait with the name of a compiler-known trait, or\na type with the name of a compiler-known type. The\ntwo are different declarations. A name you write in this module (a bound, an\nimpl head, a supertrait, a @derive argument) names the local trait. A\nform the compiler writes for you still uses the compiler-known trait:\nthe operators, Map and Set keys, assert_eq and string interpolation.\nThe warning lists those forms for the shadowed trait.\n\n  trait Eq \{                     // W1010 -- shadows `Eq` (blink.core)\n      fn eq(self, other: Self) -> Bool\n  \}\n\nSo `[T: Eq]` does not make `a == b` legal, and `@derive(Eq)` is\nNotDerivable (E1402).\n\nFix: use a different name for the local trait, or import the\ncompiler-known trait under another name and use that name:\n\n  import blink.core.\{Eq as CoreEq\}\n\n  @derive(CoreEq) type P \{ x: Int \}    // OK\n\nThe same holds for a `type`, type alias, `trait` or `effect` that takes\nthe name of a compiler-known type other than a reserved one (E0524),\nsuch as `Option`, `Handler` or `Ptr`. The name means the declaration in\nits module. A form the compiler writes for you still uses the builtin:\n`T?`, `??` and `?` use `Option`, `..` uses `Range`, `with` uses\n`Handler`.\n\n  type Option \{ label: Str \}   // W1010 -- shadows `Option[T]` (blink.core)\n\n  import blink.core.\{Option as CoreOption\}\n  fn pick(o: CoreOption[Int]) -> Int \{ o ?? 0 \}   // OK\n\n`Ptr` and `Buf` come from blink.ffi: `import blink.ffi.\{Ptr as FfiPtr\}`."
    }
    if code == SHADOWED_VARIABLE {
        return "W0603 -- ShadowedVariable\n\nA variable in an inner scope shadows a variable with the same name\nin an outer scope.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Accidentally reusing a variable name from an enclosing scope\n  - Copy-paste without renaming\n\nFix: rename the inner variable, or prefix with `_` to suppress\nthis warning.\n\n  let x = 10\n  if true \{\n      let _x = 20  // OK -- prefixed with `_`\n  \}"
    }
    if code == DISCARDED_COLLECTION_COPY {
        return "W0606 -- DiscardedCollectionCopy\n\nA collection method that returns a new collection was called as a\nstatement, so the new collection is dropped. The receiver does not\nchange, so the call does nothing.\n\n  let mut xs = [1, 2]\n  xs.append([3])               // W0606 -- xs is still [1, 2]\n\nThe lint covers the sealed built-in collection methods only: a row\nof the ListOps, MapOps or SetOps table that does not mutate and\nreturns the receiver's own type, such as `append`, `slice` and\n`union`. It does not cover user functions or any other method, so\nthe absence of this warning does not mean a result was used.\n\nFix: use the result. Assign it back to a `let mut` binding, or bind\nit to a new name.\n\n  xs = xs.append([3])          // OK\n  let longer = xs.append([3])  // OK\n\n`let _ = xs.append([3])` drops the result on purpose and does not\nwarn."
    }
    if code == UNREACHABLE_CODE {
        return "W0700 -- UnreachableCode\n\nCode appears after an unconditional return, break, or continue\nstatement and will never execute.\n\nCommon causes:\n  - Leftover code from refactoring\n  - Early return added without removing subsequent code\n\nFix: remove the unreachable code or move it before the control\nflow statement."
    }
    if code == ARENA_EFFECT_REDUNDANT {
        return "W0701 -- ArenaEffectRedundant\n\nA function declares `! Arena` but every Arena-effectful call in its\nbody is already contained inside a `with arena \{ }` block. Because\n`with arena \{ }` is the escape boundary for the `! Arena` marker,\nthe outer annotation adds no information.\n\nFix: remove `! Arena` from the function signature, or add\n@allow(ArenaEffectRedundant) if the annotation is intentional for\ndocumentation or API stability.\n\n  fn wrap() -> Int \{                     // OK -- no marker needed\n      with arena \{ consume([1, 2]) }\n  \}\n\n  fn wrap() -> Int ! Arena \{             // W0701\n      with arena \{ consume([1, 2]) }\n  \}"
    }
    if code == NEGATIVE_ZERO_COMPARISON {
        return "W0706 -- NegativeZeroComparison\n\nAn operand of a Float comparison is a written negative zero: a `-` in\nfront of a zero float literal, with optional parentheses. -0.0 and 0.0\nare equal in the Float order and in IEEE equality, so the `-` cannot\nchange the result. Code that writes `x == -0.0` usually means a sign\ntest, but that test is also true for 0.0.\n\n                                     x = 0.0    x = -0.0\n  x == -0.0                          true       true      // W0706\n  x.ieee_eq(-0.0)                    true       true      // W0706\n  x.is_sign_negative()               false      true\n  x.to_bits() == (-0.0).to_bits()    false      true\n\nThe warning covers `==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `eq`, `ne`, `cmp` and\n`ieee_eq` on Float, and `assert_eq` and `assert_ne` when the values are\nFloat. It fires in a branch that never runs, in tests and in\n`assert_panics` bodies. A generic body does not warn.\n\nThe check reads the code as written. A `const` or `let` that holds -0.0\nis not checked, and moving -0.0 into one does not make a sign test\ncorrect. A call that compares inside its own body is not checked\neither, so `xs.contains(-0.0)` gives no warning.\n\nFix: to test the sign, use `x.is_sign_negative()`. To test for negative\nzero, use `x == 0.0 && x.is_sign_negative()`. To test for zero, write\n`0.0`. No machine fix is offered: only you know which one the code\nmeans.\n\nSuppress with @allow(NegativeZeroComparison) on the function, or set\nthe level with W0706 = \"off\" or \"error\" under [lints]. A module-scope\n`let` or `const` has no function, so only [lints] applies there."
    }
    if code == TODO_IN_CODE {
        return "W0703 -- TodoInCode\n\nA call resolves to the built-in `todo()`, which marks code that is not\nwritten yet. The call panics with `not yet implemented` if it runs, so\nthe warning lets CI find each one before a release.\n\n  fn refund(order: Order) -> Int \{\n      match order.status \{\n          Pending => 0\n          Shipped => order.total\n          Cancelled => todo(\"refund rules\")   // W0703\n      }\n  }\n\nIt fires in every context, test blocks included. A function or local\nnamed `todo` that the program declares is not the built-in and gets no\nwarning. `unreachable()` has no warning: it states that a path can\nnever run, not that code is missing.\n\nFix: write the missing code. In a test that is not finished, mark the\ntest `@skip(\"reason\")` instead.\n\n  @skip(\"waiting on the refund rules\")\n  test \"refund of a cancelled order\" \{ ... }\n\nSuppress with `@allow(TodoInCode)`, or set the level with\n`W0703 = \"off\"` or `\"error\"` under `[lints]` in blink.toml."
    }
    if code == "W0702" {
        return "W0702 -- BitwisePrecedence (retired)\n\nThis code is retired and no longer reported. It warned on a bitwise\noperator with a comparison operand. Bitwise operators and shifts now\nbind tighter than comparison, so `flags & 15 == 0` is\n`(flags & 15) == 0`, and a mix of operator groups without parentheses\nis the parse error MixedOperatorPrecedence (E1127).\n\nReplaced by: MixedOperatorPrecedence (E1127)"
    }
    if code == UNAUDITED_FFI {
        return "W0800 -- UnauditedFfi\n\nAn @ffi function is not marked @trusted, meaning it has not been\naudited for safety.\n\nFix: audit the foreign function for memory safety and correctness,\nthen add @trusted(audit: \"ID\") to suppress this warning. The\naudit identifier is required (E0836).\n\n  @ffi(\"puts\")\n  @trusted(audit: \"AUDIT-001\")\n  fn c_puts(s: Ptr[U8]) -> Int ! FFI \{ \}"
    }
    if code == PUB_FFI {
        return "E0801 -- FfiFunctionPublic\n\nAn @ffi function was declared as pub. FFI functions are unsafe and\nshould not be exposed in a module's public API.\n\nFix: remove `pub` and wrap the FFI call in a safe public function.\n\n  @ffi(\"strlen\")\n  @trusted(audit: \"AUDIT-001\")\n  fn c_strlen(s: Ptr[U8]) -> Int ! FFI \{ \}\n\n  pub fn string_length(s: Str) -> Int ! FFI \{\n      c_strlen(s.as_ptr())\n  \}"
    }
    if code == FFI_NO_EFFECTS {
        return "E0802 -- FfiNoEffects\n\nAn @ffi function has no effect row. The row on a foreign function is\na claim about code the compiler cannot see, so the compiler does not\nguess it, and a missing row is an error.\n\nFix: write the effects the foreign code has after the return type,\nor `! ()` (the empty effect row) if it has none. No tool writes the\nrow for you, because only the author knows what the foreign code does.\n\n  @ffi(\"c\", \"puts\")\n  @trusted(audit: \"LIBC-PUTS\")\n  fn c_puts(s: Ptr[U8]) -> Int ! IO\n\n  @ffi(\"c\", \"strlen\")\n  @trusted(audit: \"LIBC-STRLEN\")\n  fn c_strlen(s: Ptr[U8]) -> Int ! ()\n\nThere is no `FFI` effect, and no `@effects` annotation."
    }
    if code == CONTRACT_ON_FFI {
        return "E0803 -- ContractOnFfi\n\nA @requires or @ensures annotation was used on an @ffi function.\nContracts cannot verify foreign code behavior.\n\nFix: remove the contract annotation from the FFI function. If you\nneed contracts, add them to a safe wrapper function instead.\n\n  @ffi(\"sqrt\")\n  @trusted(audit: \"AUDIT-001\")\n  fn c_sqrt(x: Float) -> Float ! FFI \{ \}\n\n  @requires(x >= 0.0)\n  @verify(fallback: \"runtime\")\n  pub fn safe_sqrt(x: Float) -> Float ! FFI \{\n      c_sqrt(x)\n  \}"
    }
    if code == INVALID_PTR_TYPE {
        return "E0810 -- InvalidPtrTypeParam\n\nPtr[T] was used with an invalid type parameter. Only FFI-compatible\ntypes are allowed inside Ptr.\n\nValid types: Void, U8, U16, U32, U64, I8, I16, I32, I64, Int,\nFloat, Ptr, and any type annotated @ffi.struct or @ffi.opaque\n\nFix: use one of the valid FFI-compatible types.\n\n  fn good(p: Ptr[U8]) ! FFI \{ \}   // OK\n  fn bad(p: Ptr[Str]) ! FFI \{ \}    // E0810: Str is not valid"
    }
    if code == PTR_OUTSIDE_FFI {
        return "E0811 -- PtrOutsideFfiContext\n\nPtr[T], or an @ffi.struct that holds a Ptr, was used outside an FFI\nregion. An FFI region is the signature or body of an @ffi or @trusted\nfunction, or a with ffi.scope() block.\n\nFix: add @ffi or @trusted to the function, or use a safe wrapper.\n\n  @trusted(audit: \"MEM-001\")\n  fn is_empty(p: Ptr[U8]) -> Bool \{\n      p.is_null()\n  \}"
    }
    if code == MISSING_CANONICAL_HEADER {
        return "W0812 -- MissingCanonicalHeader\n\nAn @ffi.struct annotation declares a canonical header that is not\nlisted in any [native-dependencies].<dep>.headers entry of\nblink.toml. Without that declaration the build cannot guarantee the\nheader will be available on the target system, so layout asserts\nmay fail to compile out-of-tree.\n\nFix: list the header in blink.toml:\n\n  [native-dependencies]\n  libname = \{ system = true, headers = [\"poll.h\"] \}\n\nUnder --strict-struct-layout the warning is escalated to error."
    }
    if code == FFI_STRUCT_GC_FIELD {
        return "E0812 -- FfiStructGcField\n\nAn @ffi.struct field uses a GC-managed or non-FFI-compatible type.\n@ffi.struct types must mirror C structs exactly, so fields must use\nsized FFI-compatible types only.\n\nValid field types: I8/I16/I32/I64, U8/U16/U32/U64, Int, Float, F32,\nF64, Bool, Ptr[T], and other @ffi.struct types.\n\nRejected: Str, Bytes, List, Map, Set, Result, Option, traits, and\nany user-defined type without @ffi.struct. A Buf field is E0822: a Buf\nis not the C pointer the field declares, so use Ptr[T].\n\nA struct with a Ptr field, directly or through a nested @ffi.struct,\nmay appear only in an FFI region, as Ptr[T] may (E0811).\n\nFix: use a sized integer/float/bool type, or wrap the GC-managed\nvalue in a Blink-side struct outside the @ffi boundary.\n\n  @ffi.struct(header: \"sys/uio.h\", name: \"iovec\")\n  pub type IoVec \{\n      iov_base: Ptr[U8], // OK\n      iov_len: U64,      // OK\n      // name: Str,      // E0812\n  \}"
    }
    if code == FFI_OFFSET_ON_SINGLETON {
        return "E0813 -- FfiOffsetOnSingleton\n\nPtr.offset(i) was called on a Ptr produced by scope.alloc[T](),\nwhich allocates a single cell. Pointer arithmetic past cell 0 is\nundefined behavior.\n\nFix: allocate an array via scope.alloc_n[T](n), then use offset.\n\n  let p: Ptr[Pollfd] = scope.alloc_n(8)\n  let q = p.offset(3)   // OK"
    }
    if code == VOID_POINTEE_ACCESS {
        return "E0825 -- VoidPointeeAccess\n\n`.deref()` and `.write(v)` on a `Ptr[Void]` are rejected\n(sections/07_trust_modules_metadata.md §9.1.1). `Void` has no value\nrepresentation, so there is no value for `deref` to return and no value\nfor `write` to store: `*(void*)p` is a C constraint violation.\n\n  let v = handle.deref()   // E0825 -- `Void` names no value to read\n  handle.write(1)          // E0825 -- `Void` names no value to write\n\nE0825 rejects no other operation, so `Ptr[Void]` remains an opaque\nhandle: `.addr()`, `.is_null()`, `null_ptr[Void]()` and passing the\npointer to C stay legal. A `Ptr[Ptr[Void]]` cell holds a pointer,\nso its own `.deref()` and `.write(h)` are legal.\n\nFix: if you know the pointee type, declare it -- `Ptr[Int]`, `Ptr[U8]`.\nFor a C struct, use `@ffi.struct` and read fields with `p.field.read()`.\n\n  let p: Ptr[Int] = scope.alloc()\n  p.write(1)\n  let v = p.deref()   // OK"
    }
    if code == BYTES_GROW_IN_WITH_PTR {
        return "E0814 -- BytesGrowInWithPtr\n\nA growth-effecting method (push/append/concat/extend/clear/\ntruncate/resize/write_*_le/be) was called on the Bytes receiver\ninside its with_ptr closure. Growth would invalidate the pinned\nPtr[U8] aliasing the GC-managed buffer.\n\nFix: move the growth call outside the with_ptr closure.\n\n  b.with_ptr(fn(p) \{ c_read(fd, p, n) })  // OK\n  b.push(0)                                // OK after closure"
    }
    if code == PINNED_BYTES_ESCAPE {
        return "E0815 -- PinnedBytesEscape\n\nThe pinned Bytes receiver was passed as an argument inside its\nwith_ptr closure. Helper-function bodies are out of scope for the\nsyntactic no-grow check, so escapes are forbidden.\n\nFix: pass the Ptr[U8] `p` or an integer slice instead.\n\n  b.with_ptr(fn(p) \{ helper_taking_ptr(p, b.len()) })   // OK"
    }
    if code == "E0816" {
        return "E0816 -- WithPtrBodyTooComplex (retired)\n\nThis code is retired and no longer reported. It once rejected a\nBytes.with_ptr closure whose body was more than one expression. The\nspec never had that rule: the body is any `fn(Ptr[U8]) -> R`, so it\nmay hold statements before its result.\n\n  let n = b.with_ptr(fn(p) \{\n      let len = b.len()\n      len + 1\n  \})   // OK"
    }
    if code == BYTES_PTR_CAST_FORBIDDEN {
        return "E0817 -- BytesPtrCastForbidden\n\nBytes cannot be cast or coerced to Ptr[U8] outside a with_ptr\nclosure. Bytes is a GC-managed buffer and may be relocated by\nthe garbage collector; raw Ptr[U8] aliases would dangle.\n\nFix: use Bytes.with_ptr to pin the buffer for the duration of\nthe call, and pass the pinned pointer to the FFI:\n\n  b.with_ptr(fn(p) \{ c_read(fd, p, b.len()) })   // OK"
    }
    if code == FFI_SCOPE_NOT_WITH_RESOURCE {
        return "E0819 -- FfiScopeNotWithResource\n\nA value of type `FfiScope` may occur only as the resource of a\n`with ... as` block, and nowhere else. It may not be bound by a\n`let`, passed as an argument, returned, stored in a field, or\nwritten as a type argument.\n\nA scope owns a libc malloc/free arena whose extent must be lexical.\nOutside a `with` there is no block to exit, so nothing frees the\narena and every allocation through it leaks for the life of the\nprocess. The rule is stated over the TYPE, not over the\n`ffi.scope()` call, because the hazard belongs to the value:\nhowever the value reached an excluded position, the arena there\noutlives the block that would free it.\n\n  fn leaks() ! IO \{\n      let arena = ffi.scope()          // E0819 -- let-bound\n  \}\n\n  fn takes(s: FfiScope) \{ }            // E0819 -- parameter\n  type Holder \{ s: FfiScope }          // E0819 -- field\n\nFix: bind the scope as a `with` resource.\n\n  with ffi.scope() as arena \{\n      let p: Ptr[Pollfd] = arena.alloc()\n      // p is freed here, at block exit\n  \}\n\nFor one plain allocation with GC cleanup, use `alloc_ptr[T]()`\ninstead of a scope. For a pointer that must outlive the block, take\nit out of the scope first: `arena.take(p)` removes p from the\nscope's cleanup list and transfers its lifetime to the caller."
    }
    if code == MISSING_NATIVE_DEP {
        return "E0820 -- MissingNativeDep\n\nAn @ffi annotation references a C library that is not declared in\nthe [native-dependencies] section of blink.toml.\n\nFix: add the library to blink.toml:\n\n  [native-dependencies]\n  libname = \{ system = true \}"
    }
    if code == NATIVE_DEP_UNAVAILABLE_CROSS {
        return "E0821 -- NativeDepUnavailableCrossTarget\n\nA native dependency declared as system-only cannot be resolved for\na cross-compilation target. Cross-compilation requires vendored\nsource or a static archive.\n\nFix: provide vendored source:\n  libname = \{ path = \"vendor/libname.c\" \}\n\nOr explicitly opt in to dynamic linking on the target:\n  libname = \{ system = true, link = \"dynamic\" \}"
    }
    if code == BUF_OUTSIDE_FFI_SURFACE {
        return "E0822 -- BufOutsideFfiSurface\n\n`Buf[T]` is the byte bridge's own type, and it is nameable only on the\nFFI surface: `@ffi.fn` parameter and return types. An inferred binding\nmay hold one, because the typechecker computes that type and nobody\nwrites it. Writing the name anywhere else -- an annotated binding, a\nparameter or return type in Blink code, a struct field, an @ffi.struct\nfield, a generic bound -- is rejected. In an @ffi.struct field a Buf\nwould be a `blink_buf_t*`, not the C pointer the field declares: use\n`Ptr[T]` there.\n\nThe buffer and its payload are malloc'd, tied to the enclosing\n`ffi.scope`, and freed when that scope unwinds. Naming the type outside\nthe bridge lets it outlive the scope that frees it, so the restriction\nis what keeps the lifetime purely lexical.\n\nFix: for a byte payload, use the helper that returns `Bytes`.\n\n  let bs = libc.recv_bytes(fd, 4096)     // OK -- also read_bytes,\n                                         //       getentropy_bytes\n\nFix: for a typed scope-tied region, use `scope.alloc_n[T]`.\n\n  let p = scope.alloc_n[Pollfd](8)       // OK\n\nSee `blink doc bytes-bridge` for the whole byte bridge."
    }
    if code == EMPTY_ROW_OUTSIDE_FFI {
        return "E0564 -- EmptyRowOutsideFfi\n\n`! ()` is the empty effect row written out. It means no effects, the\nsame as no row. It is legal only on an `@ffi` decl, where a row is a\nclaim the author must write. On any other function, fn type or trait\nmethod signature the compiler proves purity, so no row already means\nno effects.\n\n  fn add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int ! () \{   // E0564\n      a + b\n  \}\n\nFix: remove `! ()`.\n\n  fn add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int \{\n      a + b\n  \}"
    }
    if code == EFFECTS_ANNOTATION_REMOVED {
        return "E1139 -- EffectsAnnotationRemoved\n\nThere is no `@effects` annotation. The effect row goes after `!` in\nthe signature, on foreign and Blink functions alike. On an `@ffi`\ndecl with `@effects` and no row this is the one error, not E0802 as\nwell.\n\n  @ffi(\"c\", \"getpid\")\n  @effects(IO)                       // E1139\n  @trusted(audit: \"LIBC-GETPID\")\n  fn c_getpid() -> Int\n\nFix: remove `@effects(...)` and write the row after the return type.\nOn an `@ffi` decl whose foreign code has no effects, write `! ()`.\nNo tool moves the row for you: the row on a foreign function is a\nclaim that only the author can make.\n\n  @ffi(\"c\", \"getpid\")\n  @trusted(audit: \"LIBC-GETPID\")\n  fn c_getpid() -> Int ! IO"
    }
    if code == INSUFFICIENT_HANDLER_COVERAGE {
        return "E0563 -- InsufficientHandlerCoverage\n\nA handler of a sub-effect was given where a handler of its parent\neffect is expected. A `Handler[DB.Read]` handles only the operations\nof DB.Read, so it cannot stand in for a `Handler[DB]`, which must\nhandle read, write and admin.\n\n  fn run_full(h: Handler[DB]) \{ ... \}\n  run_full(read_handler)   // E0563 -- read_handler is a Handler[DB.Read]\n\nThe other way is allowed. A `Handler[DB]` given where a\n`Handler[DB.Read]` is expected converts to a handler of DB.Read:\n\n  fn read_only_test(h: Handler[DB.Read]) \{ ... \}\n  read_only_test(full)     // OK -- full is a Handler[DB]\n\nFix: pass a handler of the parent effect, or change the parameter\nto the sub-effect that the function uses."
    }
    if code == WITH_ITEM_NOT_A_HANDLER {
        return "E0839 -- NonHandlerWithItem\n\nA `with` item must be a `Handler[E]`, which installs an effect\nhandler for the block, or a scoped value: a type that implements\n`BlockHandler`, which wraps the block in its enter() and exit().\nA type that implements `Closeable` is a scoped value too: its exit\ncalls close(). The rule is the same with or without `as`: `as` only binds a\nname, to the Handler or to the scoped value's Context. Any other\nvalue has nothing to install and nothing to run.\n\n  with 42 as n \{ ... \}             // E0839 -- an Int is no handler\n  with open_db(path) as db \{ ... \} // E0839 -- a Result; add `?`\n\nA type parameter is a scoped value only when a bound makes it one.\n\n  fn hold[T](t: T) \{ with t \{ ... \} \}            // E0839\n  fn hold[T: Closeable](t: T) \{ with t \{ ... \} \} // OK\n\nFix: pass a handler or a scoped value, or add `?` to a Result.\n\n  with make_io_handler() \{ ... \}    // Handler[IO]\n  with open_db(path)? as db \{ ... \} // a Closeable, closed at exit"
    }
    if code == FFI_OFFSET_UNKNOWN_STRIDE {
        return "E0838 -- FfiOffsetUnknownStride\n\nPtr.offset(i) steps by the size of the pointer's element type, so\nthat size must be known at compile time. A sized integer, Int,\nFloat, a Ptr, or an @ffi.struct has one. Void, an @ffi.opaque\nhandle, and a type parameter do not.\n\nFix: point at a type with a size, such as Ptr[I32] or a Ptr[T]\nwhere T is annotated @ffi.struct.\n\n  @ffi.struct(header: \"poll.h\", name: \"pollfd\")\n  pub type Pollfd \{ fd: I32, events: I16, revents: I16 \}\n  let p: Ptr[Pollfd] = scope.alloc_n(8)\n  let q = p.offset(3)   // OK -- stride = sizeof(pollfd)"
    }
    if code == INVALID_LINT_LEVEL {
        return "E0843 -- InvalidLintLevel\n\nA [lints] value in blink.toml is not one of the three levels:\n\"off\", \"warn\" or \"error\" (sections/04 §4.16.8).\n\n  [lints]\n  W0551 = \"loud\"              # E0843 -- invalid lint level `loud`\n  W0601 = \"warning\"           # E0843 -- the level is \"warn\"\n\nThe error is refused, not ignored: no @allow, [lints] entry or\n--strict changes it. A setting that silenced it could hide the very\ntypo it reports, and the lint it names would keep its default level\nwith no sign that the line did nothing.\n\nFix: write one of the three levels.\n\n  [lints]\n  W0551 = \"off\"              # OK"
    }
    if code == INCOMPATIBLE_STDLIB_ARCHIVE {
        return "E0840 -- IncompatibleStdlibArchive\n\nThe installed stdlib archive (libblink_std.a in\n<prefix>/share/blink/) was built by a different blink toolchain\nthan the one now linking your program. The archive and the freshly\nemitted C disagree on struct layout / runtime ABI; linking them\nwould produce a binary that reads boxed values at the wrong offsets\nand corrupts memory at runtime (dangling reads, SIGSEGV).\n\nThe archive carries a `.archive-id` stamp (runtime.h SHA |\nlibblink_std.h SHA | compiler SHA | version | monolith.c SHA).\nruntime.h and libblink_std.h together define every struct layout\nthe user TU shares with the archive. The compiler SHA names the\nblink binary that emitted the archive's function bodies, which can\nchange while both headers stay the same. A cross build (-T) compiles\nthe shipped monolith.c for its target in place of the archive, so\nmonolith.c must be the one the archive was built from. When the\nheaders, the running blink binary or monolith.c do not match the\nstamp, the link is rejected before a binary is produced.\n\nFix: reinstall the stdlib so the archive matches the compiler:\n\n  task install\n\nOr rebuild against the current toolchain. If you see this from a\nrelease tarball, the tarball's bin/ and share/blink/ are mismatched\n-- reinstall a consistent release."
    }
    if code == CLEANUP_PANICKED_DURING_UNWIND {
        return "E0824 -- CleanupPanickedDuringUnwind\n\nA `BlockHandler.exit(false)` body or `Closeable.close()` body itself\npanicked while running on a catchable-unwind path (`?` propagation,\n`return`, assertion failure, or `skip()`). The original panic is the\nload-bearing failure — the test runner reports it as the test's\nresult and preserves it as the unwind cause. The cleanup panic is\nrecorded as a secondary warning on the same trace frame so the\nauthor can fix the cleanup bug without losing the primary cause.\n\nOn a stacked `with...as` chain, remaining handlers in the LIFO stack\nstill run (continue-drain ordering, per the multi-cleanup test\nregistrar decision); short-circuiting would leak resources held by\ndeeper-stacked handlers.\n\nFix: make the cleanup body robust to partially-initialized state.\nGuard mutating calls behind `if !ok \{ ... }` if they only make\nsense on the success path, and avoid raising new panics from inside\nan `exit`/`close` body.\n\n  type Tx \{ committed: Bool }\n  impl BlockHandler for Tx \{\n      fn exit(self, ok: Bool) \{\n          if ok \{ self.commit() } else \{ self.rollback() }\n          // a panic in commit/rollback here surfaces as E0824 warning\n      }\n  }"
    }
    if code == SKIP_OUTSIDE_TEST {
        return "E0827 -- SkipOutsideTest\n\nskip() is a test-only helper that marks the current test as skipped\nand triggers Result-sentinel propagation back to the test runner. It\nis only meaningful inside a `test \{ ... }` block; calling it from\nmain(), a regular function, or any non-test context is rejected.\n\nFix: move the call into a test block, or use a different control-flow\nprimitive (return / Err / exit) outside tests.\n\n  test \"network-bound\" \{\n      if !have_network() \{ skip(\"requires network\") }\n      // ... test body ...\n  \}\n\n  fn helper() \{ skip(\"nope\") }  // E0827 -- not in a test body"
    }
    if code == ASSERT_PANICS_BODY_RETURNED {
        return "E0831 -- AssertPanicsBodyReturned\n\nThe `assert_panics` block returned normally instead of panicking.\n`assert_panics` requires its body to terminate with a panic\n(a failed `.unwrap()`, an out-of-bounds index, a sized-integer\noverflow, or any other runtime trap). A normal return is a test\nfailure. A `?`-propagated `Err` that exits the body without\npanicking is also a body-returned failure, not the expected panic.\n\nFix: ensure the body actually triggers the expected panic, or\nremove the `assert_panics` wrapper if no panic is expected.\n\n  test \"divides by zero panics\" \{\n      assert_panics \{\n          let xs: [Int] = []\n          let _ = xs.get(0).unwrap()   // expected panic\n      }\n  }"
    }
    if code == ASSERT_PANICS_MESSAGE_MISMATCH {
        return "E0832 -- AssertPanicsMessageMismatch\n\nThe `assert_panics(matching: \"...\")` block did panic, but the panic\nmessage did not contain the `matching:` substring. The failure\noutput renders the expected substring, the full actual panic\nmessage, and the source location where the panic fired, so you can\ncorrect either the pattern or the panic.\n\nFix: align the `matching:` substring with the actual panic message,\nor the panic message with the test's intent.\n\n  assert_panics(matching: \"index out of bounds\") \{\n      let xs: [Int] = []\n      let _ = xs.get(0).unwrap()\n  }"
    }
    if code == ASSERT_PANICS_OUTSIDE_TEST {
        return "E0833 -- AssertPanicsOutsideTest\n\n`assert_panics` is a test-only construct, like `skip()` and the\nother `assert_*` matchers. Using it in `main()`, a library\nfunction, or any non-test context is rejected at the typecheck\nlayer.\n\nFix: move it into a `test \{ ... }` block.\n\n  test \"bad input panics\" \{\n      assert_panics \{ parse(\"not a number\").unwrap() }\n  }\n\n  fn helper() \{ assert_panics \{ ... } }  // E0833 -- not in a test"
    }
    if code == ASSERT_PANICS_NESTED_EXPECT_PANIC {
        return "E0834 -- AssertPanicsNestedExpectPanic\n\nAn `assert_panics` block is lexically nested inside another\n`assert_panics` block. Nesting is rejected because the outer block\nwould swallow the inner's mechanism and the semantics of which\npanic counts as \"the\" expected panic become ambiguous.\n\nFix: split the assertions into two separate tests.\n\n  test \"a\" \{ assert_panics \{ foo() } }\n  test \"b\" \{ assert_panics \{ bar() } }     // not nested -- OK\n\n  test \"bad\" \{\n      assert_panics \{\n          assert_panics \{ baz() }          // E0834\n      }\n  }"
    }
    if code == XFAIL_MISSING_REASON {
        return "E0835 -- XfailMissingReason\n\n`test.failing(...)` requires a `reason:` that is a non-empty string\nliteral. The reason says why the test is expected to fail today, and\nthe test report carries it as `xfail_reason`. To point at tracked\nwork, write the reference in the reason.\n\nFix: supply a non-empty reason.\n\n  test.failing(\n      \"trait impl resolves through alias chain\",\n      reason: \"Phase 3 trait elaboration not yet implemented\",\n  ) \{\n      // ... test body, expected to fail today\n  }"
    }
    if code == TRUSTED_REQUIRES_AUDIT {
        return "E0836 -- TrustedRequiresAudit\n\n`@trusted` is the sole channel that suppresses the audit-gated\ndiagnostics of section 9.1 (UnauditedFfi, RawBypassesParam). The\n`audit:` identifier is the record the channel exists to create. A\nbare `@trusted`, or one with an empty `audit:`, silences the\ndiagnostic and records nothing -- the one outcome the annotation\nis there to prevent.\n\nFix: give the audit a non-empty identifier.\n\n  @ffi(\"sqlite3\", \"sqlite3_close\")\n  @trusted(audit: \"DB-004\")\n  fn sqlite3_close(db: Ptr[Void]) -> Int ! IO\n\n  @trusted                             // E0836\n  fn other(db: Ptr[Void]) -> Int\n\nThe identifier is free-form: a ticket id, a review id, or a short\nnote on what was audited and why it is safe."
    }
    if code == DEPRECATED_USAGE {
        return "W2000 -- DeprecatedUsage\n\nA function or type marked @deprecated was used.\n\nThe @deprecated annotation signals that an API is scheduled for\nremoval. The warning includes the deprecation edition (if known)\nand a suggested replacement.\n\nFix: migrate to the replacement API shown in the help message.\n\n  @deprecated(replacement: \"new_fn\")\n  fn old_fn() \{ \}\n\n  fn main() \{\n      old_fn()   // W2000: use of deprecated function `old_fn`\n                 // help: use `new_fn` instead\n  \}"
    }
    if code == CONTRACT_PREDICATE_NOT_DECIDABLE {
        return "E1300 -- ContractPredicateNotDecidable\n\nA @requires/@ensures/@where predicate uses a construct outside the\nSMT-decidable subset. Predicates must be promotable to compile-time\nproofs, so they are restricted to: literals, identifiers, field/index\nreads, ranges, comparisons, boolean/arithmetic operators, if/match,\nand calls to @pure functions.\n\nDisallowed: let bindings, assignments, closures, loops, with-blocks,\nasync/await, channels, allocation, and `?`/try.\n\nFix: rewrite the predicate as a pure expression. Move side-effecting\nlogic into the function body."
    }
    if code == EFFECTFUL_CALL_IN_PREDICATE {
        return "E1301 -- EffectfulCallInPredicate\n\nA contract predicate calls a function that declares effects. Predicates\nmust be evaluable without side effects so they can be reasoned about\nstatically.\n\nFix: call only @pure functions inside predicates, or compute the value\nin the function body and reference it via `result`/`old(_)`."
    }
    if code == IMPURE_CALL_IN_PREDICATE {
        return "E1302 -- ImpureCallInPredicate\n\nA contract predicate calls a function that is not marked @pure. Even\nif the call has no declared effects, predicates must call only\nexplicitly @pure helpers (or a small bootstrap whitelist: len,\nis_empty, unwrap, abs).\n\nFix: mark the helper @pure if it qualifies, or inline the computation."
    }
    if code == LOOP_IN_PREDICATE {
        return "E1303 -- LoopInPredicate\n\nA contract predicate uses `while`, `for`, or `loop`. Loops can fail\nto terminate and have no first-order encoding, so predicates must be\nloop-free.\n\nFix: replace iteration with a recursive @pure helper or a quantifier-\nfree expression over the input."
    }
    if code == RESULT_OUTSIDE_ENSURES {
        return "E1304 -- ResultOutsideEnsures\n\nThe identifier `result` is reserved for use inside @ensures predicates,\nwhere it refers to the function's return value. It is not bound in\n@requires, @where, or function bodies.\n\nFix: use `result` only inside @ensures(...). Rename the local if you\nintended a regular variable."
    }
    if code == OLD_OUTSIDE_ENSURES {
        return "E1305 -- OldOutsideEnsures\n\n`old(expr)` is a postcondition-only construct that snapshots `expr` at\nfunction entry. It is meaningful only inside @ensures.\n\nFix: move the `old(_)` reference into an @ensures predicate, or\ncapture the entry value with a local binding before mutation."
    }
    if code == ASSIGNMENT_IN_PREDICATE {
        return "E1306 -- AssignmentInPredicate\n\nA contract predicate contains an assignment (`=` or compound `+=`\netc.). Predicates must be referentially transparent — they may not\nmutate state.\n\nFix: rewrite as a pure comparison. If the value is needed elsewhere,\nbind it before the contract or inside the function body."
    }
    if code == IMPURE_BODY_FOR_PURE_ANNOTATION {
        return "E1307 -- ImpureBodyForPureAnnotation\n\nA function marked @pure contains a construct that is not pure: an\nassignment, a mutating method call (push, pop, append, clear, insert,\nremove, set), a loop (while/for/loop), an FFI call, allocation of a\nList/Map/Set, or a call into another function that isn't @pure or on\nthe bootstrap whitelist (len, is_empty, unwrap, abs, fabs).\n\n@pure also forbids declared effects on the function signature.\n\nFix: rewrite the body without the offending construct, or remove\n@pure from the signature."
    }
    if code == CONTRACT_UNVERIFIABLE {
        return "V0003 -- ContractUnverifiable\n\nA @requires or @ensures contract could not be proved or disproved.\nThe compiler has no SMT solver yet, so every contract is unverifiable.\nA contract the compiler cannot verify does not compile unless its\nfunction says what to do with it.\n\nFix: options:\n  1. Simplify the contract predicate\n  2. Add @verify(fallback: \"runtime\") to insert a runtime check\n  3. Add @verify(fallback: \"trust\") to accept without verification\n\n  @requires(amount > 0)\n  @verify(fallback: \"runtime\")\n  fn withdraw(balance: Int, amount: Int) -> Int \{\n      balance - amount\n  \}"
    }
    if code == MODIFIES_ARG_NOT_SIMPLE_PATH {
        return "E1308 -- ModifiesArgNotSimplePath\n\nAn @modifies argument is not a simple path. @modifies tracks which\nbindings a function may mutate, so each argument must name a path\nrooted at a parameter or self: `x`, `x.field`, `x.field.inner`.\n\nDisallowed: function calls, arithmetic, indexing with computed keys,\nor expressions that produce new values.\n\nFix: pass each mutated path explicitly, or restructure the contract\nto annotate the receiver and field separately.\n\n  @modifies(buf, buf.len)   // OK\n  @modifies(buf.get(i))     // E1308 -- call expression"
    }
    if code == MAP_KEY_NOT_HASHABLE {
        return "E1400 -- MapKeyNotHashable\n\nThe key type of a Map[K, V] (or the element type of a Set[T])\ndoes not implement Hash. Two distinct shapes trigger this:\n\nFloat/Double: not hashable in Blink — NaN does not compare equal to\nitself, and two distinct float bit patterns can compare equal, so\nthey cannot satisfy the Hash + Eq contract a key/element requires.\n\nContainers and other non-derivable builtins: List/Map/Set/Option/\nResult, Bytes, StringBuilder, and fn types have no Hash impl in v1\nand cannot gain one via @derive — only builtin scalars\n(Int/sized-int/Bool/Char/Str), a tuple of hashable types, and\nuser structs/enums with @derive(Hash, Eq) are hashable.\n\nFix: for a Float key, round it to an Int (or another integer width)\nor use a stable surrogate key. For a container/non-derivable key or\nelement, use a hashable scalar or a @derive(Hash, Eq) struct/enum\ninstead.\n\n  let m: Map[F64, Int] = Map()          // E1400 -- Float\n  let m: Map[Int, Int] = Map()          // OK\n  let s: Set[Option[Int]] = Set()       // E1400 -- Option has no Hash\n  let s: Set[Int] = Set()               // OK"
    }
    if code == "E1112" {
        return "E1112 -- UnknownDerive (retired)\n\nThis code is retired and no longer reported. It matched a @derive\nargument against a list of names by spelling. A @derive argument now\nresolves by scope, like a bound, and an argument that does not denote\na derivable compiler-known trait is NotDerivable (E1402).\n\nReplaced by: NotDerivable (E1402)"
    }
    if code == NOT_DERIVABLE {
        return "E1402 -- NotDerivable\n\nA @derive argument does not denote a trait the compiler derives. The\nargument is a name you write, so it resolves by scope, as a bound or an\nimpl head does. @derive takes the compiler-known Eq, Ord, Hash, Clone,\nDebug, Serialize and Deserialize. A user trait, a local trait that\nshadows one of those, a type, or a name with no declaration is not\nderivable.\n\n  trait Eq \{ fn eq(self, other: Int) -> Bool \}\n\n  @derive(Eq) type P \{ x: Int \}       // E1402 -- `Eq` here is the local trait\n\nFix: to derive the compiler-known trait in a module that shadows it,\nimport it under another name and derive that name:\n\n  import blink.core.\{Eq as CoreEq\}\n\n  @derive(CoreEq) type P \{ x: Int \}   // OK\n\nFor a user trait, write the impl by hand.\n\nThis error is about the argument. When the argument is derivable but a\nfield's type does not implement the trait, the error is\nNonDerivableTrait (E1401)."
    }
    if code == NON_DERIVABLE_TRAIT {
        return "E1401 -- NonDerivableTrait\n\nA field of a @derive(Hash/Eq/Ord/Serialize) type has a type that does\nnot implement the derived trait, so the compiler cannot synthesize the\nmethod. For Eq a field needs what `==` needs of its type: a\ncollection, Option, Result or tuple field is Eq when its parts are, and\nthe error names the innermost type with no Eq. For Hash/Ord: collection\ntypes (List/Map/Set/Option/Result) have no hash or ordering in v1, so\nthey cannot appear under those derives; Float is not Hashable (NaN and\nbit-pattern hazards); Bool is not Orderable. For Serialize the\nv1 to_json emitter renders only Int/Float/Bool/Str, a List of a\nserializable element, and a struct/enum that itself derives Serialize —\nChar, the sized ints, F32/F64, a Ptr, an @ffi.opaque handle, or an\nOption/Map/Set/Result field is rejected rather than silently serialized\nto null. An enum-variant field is more restricted: no List, and only a\nstruct (not an enum) field may derive-recurse. A user type must itself\nderive (or impl) the same trait.\n\nFix: drop the offending field from the derive, implement the trait\nby hand, or give the field a type that derives it.\n\n  @derive(Hash)      type S \{ m: Map\\[Str, Int\\] \}   // E1401 -- Map not Hash\n  @derive(Ord)       type T \{ b: Bool \}             // E1401 -- Bool not Ord\n  @derive(Serialize) type W \{ p: Ptr\\[U8\\] \}         // E1401 -- Ptr not Serialize\n  @derive(Eq)        type V \{ w: List\\[Widget\\] \}   // E1401 -- Widget not Eq\n  @derive(Eq)        type U \{ n: List\\[Int\\] \}      // OK\n\nThis error is about a field. A @derive argument that does not denote a\nderivable trait at all is NotDerivable (E1402)."
    }
    if code == TEMPLATE_MISMATCH {
        return "E0310 -- TemplateMismatch\n\nA string template parameter type does not match the value supplied\nat the call site. Templates check parameter types like normal\nfunction calls.\n\nFix: convert the value to the expected type, or change the template\nparameter type to match.\n\n  let s = template\"hello \{name: Str\}\"\n  s.fill(name: 42)   // E0310 -- expected Str, got Int"
    }
    if code == MISSING_KEYWORD_ARG {
        return "E0510 -- MissingKeywordArg\n\nA keyword parameter of the callee received no argument at all. Every\nparameter declared after `--` needs an argument, and that argument\ncarries the parameter's label.\n\nContrast E0529 UnlabeledKeywordArg: there the value is present and\nunlabelled, and the repair annotates an argument already written. Here\nthe value is absent, and the repair inserts one.\n\nFix: supply the argument, labelled.\n\n  fn transfer(amount: Int, -- from: Int, to: Int) \{ ... \}\n  transfer(300, from: 1)             // E0510 -- `to` received nothing\n  transfer(300, from: 1, to: 2)      // OK\n\nA keyword parameter that declares a default value is never missing: the\ncall is filled from the declaration. Giving the parameter a default is\ntherefore the other repair, and it is the right one when most callers\nwant the same value.\n\n  fn transfer(amount: Int, -- from: Int, to: Int = 0) \{ ... \}\n  transfer(300, from: 1)             // OK -- `to` is 0\n\nThe default must be a const expression, and only a parameter declared\nafter `--` may carry one. See E0532 and E0531."
    }
    if code == INVALID_KEYWORD_ARG {
        return "E0511 -- InvalidKeywordArg\n\nA call-site label names no keyword parameter of the callee. A label\nresolves in the keyword-parameter namespace -- the parameters declared\nafter `--` -- and nothing else, so a misspelling and a label written on\na positional parameter are both refused here.\n\nFix: correct the label to one the signature declares, or drop it when\nthe parameter it names is positional.\n\n  fn transfer(amount: Int, -- from: Int, to: Int) \{ ... \}\n  transfer(300, frm: 1, to: 2)       // E0511 -- no keyword parameter `frm`\n  transfer(amount: 300, from: 1, to: 2) // E0511 -- `amount` is positional\n  transfer(300, from: 1, to: 2)      // OK\n\nA variant paren payload takes the same order (sections/03_types.md\n§3.4): its positional arguments come first.\n\n  type Figure \{ Circle(radius: Float, tag: Int) \}\n  Figure.Circle(tag: 2, 1.0)         // E0527\n  Figure.Circle(1.0, tag: 2)         // OK"
    }
    if code == POSITIONAL_AFTER_KEYWORD {
        return "E0527 -- PositionalAfterKeyword\n\nA positional argument follows a labelled one. Positional arguments fill\nthe parameters declared before `--`, in order, so one written after a\nlabel has no slot left to take it.\n\nFix: move it before the first label, or give it the label its parameter\ndeclares.\n\n  fn transfer(amount: Int, -- from: Int, to: Int) \{ ... \}\n  transfer(300, from: 1, 2)          // E0527\n  transfer(300, from: 1, to: 2)      // OK"
    }
    if code == DUPLICATE_KEYWORD_ARG {
        return "E0528 -- DuplicateKeywordArg\n\nThe same label appears twice in one call. Keyword arguments are\norder-independent, so two arguments carrying one label hand the\nparameter two values with no rule that picks between them.\n\nFix: delete the repeated argument.\n\n  fn transfer(amount: Int, -- from: Int, to: Int) \{ ... \}\n  transfer(300, from: 1, from: 2)    // E0528\n  transfer(300, from: 1, to: 2)      // OK"
    }
    if code == UNLABELED_KEYWORD_ARG {
        return "E0529 -- UnlabeledKeywordArg\n\nA keyword parameter received its argument positionally. The `--`\nseparator exists to make the swap in `transfer(300, bob, alice)`\nimpossible; an unlabelled value in a keyword slot is that swap, written\nwithout the label that would have caught it.\n\nFix: label the argument already written. Both the parameter names and\ntheir positions are known here, so this is the one call-shape repair a\ntool can apply unaided.\n\n  fn transfer(amount: Int, -- from: Int, to: Int) \{ ... \}\n  transfer(300, 1, 2)                // E0529 -- `from` and `to` are keyword parameters\n  transfer(300, from: 1, to: 2)      // OK"
    }
    if code == CLOSEABLE_ESCAPES_SCOPE {
        return "E0601 -- CloseableEscapesScope / PtrEscapesFfiScope\n\nCloseableEscapesScope: a `Closeable` value escapes the `with ... as`\nblock that owns it. Closeable resources are released when the\nwith-block exits, so references that survive would be use-after-close.\n\nFix: keep the value inside the with-block, or copy out only the\ndata you need before the block ends.\n\n  with open(\"f.txt\") as f \{\n      let bytes = f.read_all()  // OK -- bytes is plain data\n      // return f               // E0601 -- f outlives the scope\n  \}\n\nPtrEscapesFfiScope: a `Ptr` allocated inside a `with ffi.scope() as\nscope` block escapes the block. The scope frees its allocations when\nthe block exits, so the escaped Ptr would dangle.\n\nFix: call `scope.take(p)` to detach the Ptr from the scope before it\nleaves the block.\n\n  with ffi.scope() as scope \{\n      let p: Ptr[Pollfd] = scope.alloc()\n      scope.take(p)             // OK -- detached, may now escape\n  \}\n\nTag-mismatch: a Ptr from an inner scope is stored into a cell declared\nin an outer scope. The inner scope frees the pointer first, so the\nouter cell would dangle. The error names the store form.\n\nWrite: `.write()` stores into the pointee.\n\n  with ffi.scope() as outer \{\n      let pp = outer.alloc[Ptr[U8]]()\n      with ffi.scope() as inner \{\n          pp.write(inner.alloc[U8]())     // E0601 -- write\n      \}\n  \}\n\nAssignment: `place = v` or `place op= v` stores into the root `let mut`\nof the place. A store into `v.iov_base` or `t.0` is a store into `v` or\n`t`, and `v` or `t` has the scope around its declaration.\n\n  with ffi.scope() as outer \{\n      let mut v = IoVec \{ iov_base: outer.alloc[U8](), iov_len: 1 \}\n      let mut t = (outer.alloc[U8](), 1)\n      with ffi.scope() as inner \{\n          v.iov_base = inner.alloc[U8]()  // E0601 -- assignment to a field path\n          t.0 = inner.alloc[U8]()         // E0601 -- assignment to a tuple field\n      \}\n  \}\n\nCapture: a closure that captures a Ptr from `inner` may not be stored\nin a cell that `inner` does not enclose.\n\n  with ffi.scope() as outer \{\n      let mut f = fn() \{ 0 \}\n      with ffi.scope() as inner \{\n          let p: Ptr[U8] = inner.alloc[U8]()\n          f = fn() \{ if p.is_null() \{ 1 \} else \{ 0 \} \}  // E0601 -- capture\n      \}\n  \}\n\nCall: a Ptr passed next to an argument whose parameter type can hold\npointers (`Ptr[Ptr[T]]`, `Ptr[S]` for a struct with a `Ptr` field, or\na `List`, `Map` or `Set` of pointers) counts as stored into that\nargument. A method receiver is an argument, so `xs.push(v)` and\n`m.insert(k, v)` are call stores. Two `Ptr[U8]` arguments never pair.\n\n  with ffi.scope() as outer \{\n      let iov = outer.alloc_n[IoVec](2)\n      let mut ps: List[Ptr[U8]] = []\n      with ffi.scope() as inner \{\n          let buf = inner.alloc_n[U8](64)\n          set_iov(iov, buf, 64)           // E0601 -- call\n          ps.push(buf)                    // E0601 -- call\n      \}\n  \}\n\nFix: allocate the value from the cell's scope (`outer.alloc`), declare\nthe cell inside the inner scope, or detach the value with\n`inner.take(p)`.\n\nThese checks see only stores the Blink program makes. A C function\nthat keeps a pointer after it returns (`aio_write`, an `io_uring`\nsubmission, `setvbuf`) is part of the audited trust base, the same as\nan `@trusted` body. `blink audit` lists each `@ffi` function that takes\na pointer-bearing argument."
    }
    if code == MUTATION_REQUIRES_MUT {
        return "E0610 -- MutationRequiresMut\n\nA mutation needs a mutable root: the first name of the receiver or of\nthe place must be `let mut`, a `mut` pattern binder or a `mut`\nparameter. A mutation is a call to a method that declares `mut self`\n(List `push`, `pop`, `set`, `clear`; Map and Set `insert`, `remove`,\n`clear`; the `StringBuilder` and `Bytes` writers), or an assignment\n`=` or `op=`. Each mutation source is then visible at its binding.\n\nAn assignment to a parameter, or to a field of one, is E0612\nParamAssign, with or without `mut`.\n\nFix: declare the binding `let mut`. A module-level `let` gets no\nautomatic fix, since `mut` adds `State` to the row of each function\nthat reads or writes it; `pub let mut` is not allowed (E1013).\n\n  let names = [\"Alice\"]\n  names.push(\"Dave\")       // E0610 -- names is not mut\n  let total = 0\n  total += 1               // E0610\n\n  let mut names = [\"Alice\"]\n  names.push(\"Dave\")       // OK\n  let mut total = 0\n  total += 1               // OK"
    }
    if code == NOT_A_PLACE {
        return "E0611 -- NotAPlace\n\nThe left side of `=` or `op=` is not a place: a step of the path goes\nthrough a `Ptr` (sections/02_syntax.md §2.22,\nsections/07_trust_modules_metadata.md §9.1.3). The `Ptr` can be the\nroot or a field in the middle of the path, and the root kind does not\nmatter: a `let`, a `let mut` and a parameter all give E0611.\nA field behind a `Ptr` is written with `.write()`.\n\n  let p = scope.alloc[PollFd]()\n  p.events = POLLIN            // E0611\n  p.events.write(POLLIN)       // OK\n  p.events += 1                // E0611\n  p.events.write(p.events.read() + 1)   // OK, a read and then a write\n\n`place = rhs` runs `rhs` before it reads the root, but `p.f.write(rhs)`\nreads `p` first. When `rhs` runs code, bind it to a name on the line\nbefore, then write the name."
    }
    if code == PARAM_ASSIGN {
        return "E0612 -- ParamAssign\n\nAn assignment `=` or `op=` whose root is a parameter, `self` included,\nwith or without `mut`. A parameter holds this function's own copy of\nthe value, so the write never reaches the caller. A `mut` parameter is\na mutable root only for a call to a `mut self` method.\n\nFix: copy the parameter to a new `let mut` name and change the copy,\nor build a new value and return it.\n\n  fn bump(c: Counter) \{\n      c.at = 5                 // E0612 -- the caller never sees it\n  \}\n\n  fn bump(c: Counter) -> Counter \{\n      let mut x = c\n      x.at = 5                 // OK\n      x\n  \}\n\nParamAssign is a warning for one release, then an error."
    }
    if code == MUT_ON_PATTERN {
        return "E0613 -- MutOnPattern\n\n`mut` marks the one name right after it. Before a pattern that is not a\nname -- a tuple, list, struct, constructor, variant, `_`, a literal or a\nrange -- it marks nothing. Before a field label it marks the label,\nwhich is not a name the pattern binds. After `with ... as` it would let\nthe name change, but `close()` runs on the value that `with` acquired.\n\nFix: put `mut` on each name inside the pattern, move it after the\ncolon of a field label, or remove it.\n\n  let (mut (a, b), c) = ((1, 2), 3)   // E0613\n  let ((mut a, mut b), c) = ((1, 2), 3)   // OK\n\n  match p \{ Point \{ mut x: px \} => px \}   // E0613\n  match p \{ Point \{ x: mut px \} => px \}   // OK\n\n  match o \{ mut None => 0, Some(v) => v \}   // E0613 -- None binds no name"
    }
    if code == MUTABLE_CAPTURE_IN_SPAWN {
        return "E0650 -- MutableCaptureInSpawn\n\nA closure passed to `async.spawn` captures a `let mut` binding. The\ntask and the scope that spawned it would share one mutable binding\nwith no order between their reads and writes, so the capture is an\nerror even when the task only reads it. A closure reached through a\n`let` counts too, since it carries its captures with it.\n\nFix: copy the value into a `let` before the spawn, or send values\nover a channel.\n\n  let mut count = 0\n  async.spawn(fn() \{ count + 1 \})      // E0650\n\n  let snapshot = count\n  async.spawn(fn() \{ snapshot + 1 \})   // OK"
    }
    if code == ARENA_TYPE_CONTAINS_CYCLE {
        return "E0701 -- ArenaTypeContainsCycle\n\nA value crossing the `with arena \{ \}` boundary has a type that\nreaches itself, directly or through a List, Map, Set, Option or\nenum payload. The arena promotion walker copies reachable cells by\ntype, and it cannot copy a value whose type is recursive.\n\nFix: build the value outside the `with arena \{ \}` block, or return a\nprojection whose type is not recursive.\n\n  type Node \{ next: Option[Node], v: Int \}\n  let n = with arena \{ Node \{ next: None, v: 1 \} \}   // E0701\n  let v = with arena \{ Node \{ next: None, v: 1 \}.v \}  // OK -- Int"
    }
    if code == ARENA_CLOSURE_TAIL_NONLITERAL {
        return "E0702a -- ArenaClosureTailNonLiteral\n\nThe tail of `with arena \{ ... \}` is a closure made in the block\nthat is not a `fn` literal written there: it comes from a call, or\nfrom a variable the block assigns again. The compiler copies only\nthe closures it can see being built, because only for those does it\nknow each capture and its type. This also applies to a closure that\nleaves the block through `return` or `?`.\n\nA closure bound outside the block, and a named fn, can pass\nthrough the tail: they do not live in the arena.\n\nFix: write the closure as a `fn` literal in the block, or bind it\nto a `let` there and return that name:\n\n  let f = with arena \{\n      let xs = [1, 2, 3]\n      fn(i: Int) -> Int \{ xs.get(i).unwrap() \}   // OK -- literal\n  \}"
    }
    if code == ARENA_CLOSURE_UNSUPPORTED_CAP {
        return "E0702d -- ArenaClosureUnsupportedCapture\n\nA closure that leaves a `with arena \{ \}` block would capture a\nvalue whose type the compiler cannot copy out of the arena. No type\nreports this today: every type a closure can capture has a walker,\nincluding a handler and a Template, so the capture is copied with the\nclosure. A capture whose type holds itself reports E0701.\n\nFix: build the closure outside the `with arena \{ \}` block."
    }
    if code == SEALED_METHOD_OVERRIDE {
        return "E0731 -- SealedMethodOverride\n\nAn `impl` overrides a trait default method declared `final`. Sealed\ndefaults are derived views of the trait's required methods, so an\noverride could disagree with the method it derives from -- two\nvalues for the same observable property.\n\n`Display.display` is sealed: the compiler synthesizes\n`display() -> Str` from your `fmt` by rendering through a fresh\nStringBuilder. Defining `display` inside `impl Display` would let\n`x.display()` drift from what `x.fmt(sb)` writes.\n\n`Eq.ne` is sealed the same way: it is always `!self.eq(other)`.\n\nFix: implement the required method and delete the override.\n\n  impl Display for Point \{\n      fn fmt(self, sb: StringBuilder) \{ sb.write(\"...\") \}  // OK\n      // fn display(self) -> Str \{ ... \}                  // E0731\n  \}"
    }
    if code == TRAIT_CONTRACT_900 {
        return "E0900 -- TraitContractMissingMethod\n\nAn `impl Trait for Type` does not implement a method required by\nthe trait. Trait contracts enforce structural conformance: every\ntrait method must appear in the impl with a matching signature.\n\nFix: add the missing method to the impl, or remove the impl if it\nshould not satisfy the trait."
    }
    if code == TRAIT_CONTRACT_901 {
        return "E0901 -- TraitContractWrongArity\n\nA method in `impl Trait for Type` has a different number of\nparameters than the trait declares.\n\nFix: align the impl method's parameter list with the trait."
    }
    if code == TRAIT_CONTRACT_902 {
        return "E0902 -- TraitContractParamMismatch\n\nA parameter type in an impl method does not match the corresponding\nparameter in the trait declaration.\n\nFix: change the impl parameter type to match the trait, or change\nthe trait if the new type is intentional."
    }
    if code == TRAIT_CONTRACT_903 {
        return "E0903 -- TraitContractReturnMismatch\n\nAn impl method's return type does not match the trait's declared\nreturn type.\n\nA trait method written with no arrow declares that it returns\nnothing, and that is part of the contract. An impl of it must also\nbe written with no arrow.\n\nFix: align the impl return type with the trait; where the trait has\nno arrow, remove the impl's arrow rather than writing a type."
    }
    if code == TRAIT_CONTRACT_904 {
        return "E0904 -- TraitContractEffectMismatch\n\nAn impl method declares effects that differ from the trait. Impls\nmay declare a subset of the trait's effects but not a superset.\n\nFix: remove the extra effects from the impl, or widen the trait\nsignature if the new effects are intentional."
    }
    if code == TRAIT_CONTRACT_905 {
        return "E0905 -- TraitContractExtraMethod\n\nAn `impl Trait for Type` declares a method that is not part of the\ntrait. Impl blocks may only contain methods named by the trait.\n\nFix: move the extra method to a free `impl Type` block (inherent\nimpl), or add it to the trait declaration."
    }
    if code == TRAIT_CONTRACT_906 {
        return "E0906 -- TraitContractGenericMismatch\n\nAn impl method's generic-parameter list does not match the trait's\ngeneric-parameter list (count, kind, or bounds).\n\nFix: align the impl method's generics with the trait declaration."
    }
    if code == TRAIT_CONTRACT_907 {
        return "E0907 -- TraitContractSealedBuiltin\n\nTwo kinds of built-in are sealed and may not be implemented:\n\n1. The method-surface traits -- StrOps, BytesOps, StringBuildOps,\n   Sized, Contains, ListOps, MapOps, SetOps, Joinable -- are part\n   of the prelude. They back the built-in method dispatch on\n   Str/List/Map/Set/Bytes; user code may neither redefine one nor\n   write an `impl` of one.\n\n2. The sealed built-in TYPES -- Str, List, Map, Set, Bytes,\n   StringBuilder, Instant, Duration, Iterator (§3c.4), plus the\n   closed collection carrier Range -- are closed. `Iterator` is\n   opaque and lazy; naming one of these in the trait position of an\n   `impl` is a sealed-type violation, not an undefined trait.\n\nFix: choose a different name for your own trait, or define your own\ntrait with the methods you need and `impl` that instead.\n\n  trait StrOps \{ fn bogus(self) -> Int }   // E0907 -- redefines a sealed builtin\n  impl Contains for MyType \{ ... }          // E0907 -- impl of a sealed builtin trait\n  impl Iterator[Int] for MyType \{ ... }     // E0907 -- impl of a sealed builtin type"
    }
    if code == POLY_IMPL_UNSUPPORTED {
        return "E0908 -- PolyImplUnsupported\n\nA polymorphic impl header was found: `impl[T] Trait for Recv`,\n`impl Trait for Recv[Args]`, or a combination. Spec §3.6 defines\nthe canonical binding form `impl[T] Trait[T] for Recv[T]`, and the\nparser accepts it, but the compiler cannot yet generate code for\npolymorphic impls. Compilation stops before codegen so nothing\nmiscompiles.\n\nFix: write a separate impl per concrete type for now.\n\n  impl[T] ListOps[T] for List[T] \{ ... }   // E0908 -- not yet compiled\n  impl Show for Box[Int] \{ ... }            // E0908 -- receiver type args\n  impl Show for IntBox \{ ... }              // OK -- concrete receiver"
    }
    if code == IMPL_BINDER_UNUSED {
        return "E0909 -- ImplBinderUnused\n\nA type-parameter binder declared after `impl` (e.g. `impl[T]`) is\nnot referenced anywhere in the impl header's type positions -- it\nappears in neither the trait's type arguments nor the receiver's\ntype arguments. An unused binder is unconstrained: no call site\ncould ever determine what type it stands for.\n\nFix: remove the binder, or use it in the header.\n\n  impl[T] Show for IntBox \{ ... }            // E0909 -- T never used\n  impl[T] Convert[T] for IntBox \{ ... }      // OK -- T binds the trait arg\n  impl[T] Show for Box[T] \{ ... }            // OK -- T binds the receiver arg"
    }
    if code == TRAIT_ARG_ARITY {
        return "E0910 -- TraitContractArgArity\n\nAn `impl` supplies the wrong number of type arguments for the\ntrait it implements. A trait declared `trait T[A, B]` has two type\nparameters, so an impl must write `impl T[X, Y] for Recv` -- one\nargument per parameter. Supplying too few leaves a trait type\nparameter unbound: the compiler has no type to check the method\nsignatures against, and cannot report a meaningful contract error.\n\nFix: supply one type argument per trait type parameter.\n\n  trait From2[T, U] \{ fn op(self, x: T, y: U) -> T }\n  impl[T] From2[T] for Box[T] \{ ... }        // E0910 -- 1 arg for 2 params\n  impl[T] From2[T, Int] for Box[T] \{ ... }   // OK -- both params bound"
    }
    if code == MISSING_SUPERTRAIT_IMPL {
        return "E0911 -- MissingSupertraitImpl\n\nA trait may declare supertraits: `trait Ranked: Ord` says every\ntype that implements Ranked must also implement Ord. This is the\nsoundness foundation of the licensing rule for `Self` in a default\nbody (E0305) -- a default body may call a supertrait's method on\n`Self` only because every implementor is guaranteed to provide it.\n\nSo an `impl` of a subtrait must satisfy each of the trait's\nsupertraits (transitively) for the receiver type. `@derive` counts\n(it auto-derives supertraits), as does an explicit `impl`. A\nbuilt-in receiver (Int, Str, ...) provides the built-in comparison\nand arithmetic traits intrinsically.\n\nFix: derive or implement the missing supertrait for the receiver.\n\n  trait Ranked: Ord \{ fn rank(self) -> Int }\n  type Foo \{ x: Int }\n  impl Ranked for Foo \{ ... }          // E0911 -- Foo does not implement Ord\n\n  @derive(Ord) type Foo \{ x: Int }     // OK -- derive provides Ord (and Eq)\n  impl Ranked for Int \{ ... }          // OK -- Int is Ord intrinsically"
    }
    if code == CIRCULAR_DEPENDENCY {
        return "E1002 -- CircularPackageDep\n\nThe lockfile contains a circular dependency between packages.\nPackage A depends on B, and B depends (transitively) on A.\n\nFix: break the cycle by extracting shared code into a third\npackage that both depend on, or remove the back-edge dependency.\n\n  // libs/A/blink.toml depends on B\n  // libs/B/blink.toml depends on A   // E1002 -- cycle"
    }
    if code == AMBIGUOUS_IMPORT {
        return "E1005 -- AmbiguousImport\n\nA bare imported name resolves to multiple modules. The compiler\ncannot pick one without explicit disambiguation.\n\nFix: rename one of the conflicting imports with `as`, or use a\nfully-qualified module path at the use site.\n\n  import http\n  import http2 as http   // E1005 -- both bind `http`"
    }
    if code == IMPORT_NOT_SELECTED {
        return "E1006 -- ImportNotSelected\n\nA selective import (`import mod.\{a, b\}`) does not list the symbol\nbeing referenced. Selective imports are documentation: the listed\nsymbols are the ones explicitly used.\n\nFix: add the symbol to the import's brace list, or reference it\nvia the module name.\n\n  import std.list.\{push\}\n  push(xs, 1)   // OK\n  pop(xs)       // E1006 -- `pop` not in list"
    }
    if code == MODULE_QUALIFIED_TYPE {
        return "E1007 -- ModuleQualifiedType\n\nA type was referenced via a module-qualified path that does not\nresolve to a public type in that module.\n\nFix: check the module path and the type name, and confirm the\ntype is `pub`.\n\n  let p: pkg.geom.Point = ...   // pkg.geom must export `Point`"
    }
    if code == RAW_OUTSIDE_TEMPLATE {
        return "E0530 -- RawOutsideTemplate\n\n`Raw[T]` is a marker type, and the only thing that consumes one is a\n`Template[C]` coercion, which folds the wrapped value into the\nadjacent literal segment of a template. A `Raw[T]` that reaches any\nother position is stranded: it implements no Display, nothing strips\nit back to the `T` it wraps, and no code will ever read it.\n\n  fn log_table(table: Str) ! IO \{\n      io.println(\"scanning \{Raw(table)}\")   // E0530\n  \}\n\nThe marker rides a binding, so the mistake is the same one line\nlater: `let t = Raw(table)` has type `Raw[Str]`, and interpolating\n`t` into an ordinary string is this error too.\n\nFix: drop the wrapper and interpolate the value itself.\n\n  io.println(\"scanning \{table}\")\n\nKeep the wrapper only where a `Template[C]` takes the literal -- a\ntemplated query, for instance -- where folding the value into the\nquery text is the point, and where it raises W0310 RawBypassesParam\nso the bypass leaves a record."
    }
    if code == TEMPLATE_HOLE_TYPE {
        return "E0534 -- TemplateHoleType\n\nA template hole takes a value a handler can bind, or a `Raw[Str]`\nthat folds into the query text. `Raw` wraps only a `Str`: the folded\ntext is the string, byte for byte, so no other type has a rule for\nits text form.\n\n  db.query(\"SELECT * FROM posts LIMIT \{Raw(n)}\")   // E0534 -- `n` is `Int`\n\nFix: send the value as a parameter.\n\n  db.query(\"SELECT * FROM posts LIMIT \{n}\")        // OK\n\nOnly when the context takes no parameter at that place does the\nauthor build the text, and then the conversion is written out:\n\n  let w = width.display()\n  db.execute(\"CREATE TABLE t (title VARCHAR(\{Raw(w)}))\")"
    }
    if code == POSITIONAL_PARAM_DEFAULT {
        return "E0531 -- PositionalParamDefault\n\nA default value was written on a positional parameter. Only the\nparameters declared after the `--` separator may carry one.\n\nA positional argument is matched by its place in the call, so every\nparameter before it must be supplied for a later one to be reached.\nA default that can never be taken unless the caller writes the value\nanyway states a rule the call site cannot use.\n\nFix: move the parameter after `--`, which also makes it keyword-required.\n\n  fn listen(host: Str = \"0.0.0.0\")        // E0531 -- positional\n  fn listen(-- host: Str = \"0.0.0.0\")     // OK\n\nOr drop the default and let every caller supply the value.\n\n  fn listen(host: Str)                    // OK"
    }
    if code == NON_CONST_EXPR {
        return "E0533 -- NonConstExpr\n\nThe right-hand side of a `const` is not a const expression. A const is\nevaluated by the compiler and its value is written in at every use, so\na right-hand side the compiler cannot evaluate leaves nothing to write.\n\nA const expression is a closed set: scalar literals, arithmetic, boolean\nand comparison operators, other `const` bindings by name, struct literals\nwhose fields are const, and enum variants with const payloads.\n\nOutside it are calls of every kind, and names bound by `let`.\n\n  const MAX = compute_max()          // E0533 -- a function call\n  const LEN = \"hello\".len()          // E0533 -- a method call\n  const BUF = Map()                  // E0533 -- a constructor allocates\n\n  let limit = read_limit()\n  const CAP = limit                  // E0533 -- `limit` is a let\n\nFix: write the value, or name another const.\n\n  const MAX = 64                     // OK\n  const BUFFER = 1024 * MAX          // OK\n\nPurity is not constness. A function with no effects still runs at run\ntime; const evaluation is the narrower question of what the compiler can\ncompute while compiling. See E0532 for the same rule on a parameter\ndefault."
    }
    if code == NON_CONST_PARAM_DEFAULT {
        return "E0532 -- NonConstParamDefault\n\nA parameter default must be a const expression: a literal, a `const`\nbinding, or arithmetic over those. This one is computed at run time.\n\nThe default stands in for an argument the caller did not write, and\nthe value it stands for is fixed by the signature, not by when the\ncall happens. An expression that can answer differently on two calls\nwould make one signature mean two things.\n\nFix: use a literal, or name a `const`.\n\n  fn listen(-- port: Int = now())         // E0532 -- computed\n  fn listen(-- port: Int = 8080)          // OK\n\n  const DEFAULT_PORT = 8080\n  fn listen(-- port: Int = DEFAULT_PORT)  // OK\n\nArithmetic over const operands is itself const.\n\n  fn window(-- ms: Int = 60 * 1000)       // OK"
    }
    if code == AUDIT_GATED_SUPPRESSION {
        return "E0837 -- AuditGatedSuppression\n\nAn audit-gated diagnostic was named in `@allow(...)` or under `[lints]`\nin blink.toml. Neither channel reaches it, and naming it there is\nrefused rather than ignored.\n\nThese diagnostics exist to force a RECORD, not to report a mistake. The\nprogram they fire on is legal and may well be correct; what the language\nasks is that a human looked at it and said so somewhere a reviewer can\nfind. `@allow` and `[lints]` silence without recording anything, so they\nwould defeat the only thing the diagnostic is for.\n\nThe refusal is an error rather than silence because a suppression that\nquietly fails to apply and one that quietly succeeds are equally bad:\nthe author cannot tell which happened.\n\nFix: write the audit on the enclosing function instead.\n\n  @allow(RawBypassesParam)              // E0837\n  @trusted(audit: \"DB-003\")              // OK -- the record the rule wants\n\nThe audit-gated list is UnauditedFfi (W0800) and RawBypassesParam\n(W0310). A diagnostic joins it only when silently suppressing it would\ndestroy a record the language promises exists."
    }
    if code == RAW_BYPASSES_PARAM {
        return "W0310 -- RawBypassesParam\n\n`Raw(...)` was used to inject text into a templated query, bypassing\nthe parameterization that prevents injection. Raw fragments must come\nfrom trusted sources.\n\nFix: pass user input through a parameter rather than `Raw`. Reserve\n`Raw` for compile-time-fixed fragments such as table or column names\nthat the parameterization machinery cannot represent.\n\n  let q = sql\"select * from \{Raw(table)\} where id = \{user_id\}\"\n\nIf the fragment is genuinely fixed and reviewed, suppress with\n`@trusted(audit: \"AUDIT-ID\")` on the enclosing function. This records\nan auditable trail, the same mechanism used for @ffi (W0800)."
    }
    ""
}
